1
00:00:26,113 --> 00:00:28,637
<i>NARRATOR:</i>
<i>Hear now the song of Troy,</i>

2
00:00:28,724 --> 00:00:32,032
<i>and of the years</i>
<i>the sea would not forget.</i>

3
00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:35,035
<i>Long ago, the kingdoms </i>
<i>of Greece united</i>

4
00:00:35,122 --> 00:00:36,732
<i>in a common cause...</i>

5
00:00:36,819 --> 00:00:38,734
<i>summoned by oath and honor.</i>

6
00:00:40,649 --> 00:00:44,653
<i>Their kings gathered</i>
<i>a mighty fleet</i>

7
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:47,873
<i>and sailed across the sea</i>
<i>to the great city of Troy.</i>

8
00:00:50,528 --> 00:00:53,488
<i>Its walls higher</i>
<i>than any siege tower,</i>

9
00:00:53,575 --> 00:00:56,665
<i> </i><i>thick enough that no battering</i><i> </i>
<i>ram could break them.</i>

10
00:00:56,752 --> 00:00:59,624
<i>A city that should</i>
<i>never have fallen.</i>

11
00:00:59,711 --> 00:01:04,934
<i>At its heart was Helen, wife</i>
<i>of Menelaus, king of Sparta,</i>

12
00:01:05,021 --> 00:01:08,285
<i>and when the Trojan prince</i>
<i>Paris carried her away,</i>

13
00:01:08,372 --> 00:01:12,376
<i>the kingdoms of Greece</i>
<i>arose as one.</i>

14
00:01:12,463 --> 00:01:18,078
<i>For ten long years,</i>
<i>they lay siege to Troy.</i>

15
00:01:18,165 --> 00:01:20,732
<i>Though countless warriors</i>
<i>fought and died,</i>

16
00:01:20,819 --> 00:01:23,692
<i> </i><i>the city remained unconquered.</i>

17
00:01:23,779 --> 00:01:27,261
<i>But where armies had failed,</i>
<i>cunning would prevail.</i>

18
00:01:27,348 --> 00:01:29,306
<i>Under the cover of darkness,</i>

19
00:01:29,393 --> 00:01:32,788
<i>Odysseus set a daring</i>
<i>plan into motion.</i>

20
00:01:32,875 --> 00:01:36,052
<i>Greek warriors entered</i>
<i>the city unseen.</i>

21
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:46,497
<i>By dawn, Troy was</i>
<i>consumed by fire,</i>

22
00:01:46,584 --> 00:01:50,240
<i>and the long war</i>
<i>had come to an end.</i>

23
00:01:50,327 --> 00:01:53,809
<i>Yet for Odysseus,</i>
<i>patient as the tide,</i>

24
00:01:53,896 --> 00:01:56,072
<i>and cunning beyond all others,</i>

25
00:01:56,159 --> 00:01:59,293
<i>the longest journey</i>
<i>was just beginning.</i>

26
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:01,686
The King!

27
00:02:01,773 --> 00:02:03,384
King Odysseus!

28
00:02:03,471 --> 00:02:06,430
Our men are gone,
but Troy is fallen,

29
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:07,997
and our battle is won.

30
00:02:08,084 --> 00:02:09,607
Where are we going?

31
00:02:09,694 --> 00:02:11,566
Home.
To my kingdom.

32
00:02:11,653 --> 00:02:14,003
To my son. My wife.

33
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:15,309
You may join me.

34
00:02:15,396 --> 00:02:17,137
Be an advisor
in my court.

35
00:02:17,224 --> 00:02:22,490
For your bravery in this
terrible war, I will ensure

36
00:02:22,577 --> 00:02:24,970
you will want for nothing
for the rest of your life.

37
00:02:25,057 --> 00:02:27,538
Thank you, my King.

38
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:29,801
It would be my honor.

39
00:02:29,888 --> 00:02:32,413
The journey will not be easy.

40
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:36,025
I will swim if I must,
but I will get home.

41
00:02:39,463 --> 00:02:40,334
Trojans!

42
00:02:42,379 --> 00:02:43,511
If the gods are willing.

43
00:03:13,236 --> 00:03:16,892
Tell me, O Muse,
the man of many devices,

44
00:03:16,979 --> 00:03:20,112
who wandered full
many ways after he had

45
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:23,681
sacked the great
citadel of Troy.

46
00:03:23,768 --> 00:03:26,206
Home, then.

47
00:03:26,293 --> 00:03:27,946
Lest I have not honored the gods

48
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:30,558
enough to receive
the reward I deserve.

49
00:03:33,474 --> 00:03:36,216
Greetings, Odysseus.

50
00:03:36,303 --> 00:03:37,608
The good man.

51
00:03:37,695 --> 00:03:38,783
Bright-eyed Athena.

52
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:41,133
Goddess on high.

53
00:03:41,221 --> 00:03:42,657
Clever, maybe.

54
00:03:42,744 --> 00:03:44,311
Clever is better than good.

55
00:03:44,398 --> 00:03:45,355
Not just clever.

56
00:03:46,748 --> 00:03:50,752
Brutal, decisive,
and ingenious.

57
00:03:50,839 --> 00:03:54,103
Hiding your soldiers
in the gift of a wooden horse,

58
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:55,800
your Trojan horse.

59
00:03:55,887 --> 00:03:58,890
Taking the city by surprise
from the inside out.

60
00:03:58,977 --> 00:04:00,196
Your greatest work.

61
00:04:00,283 --> 00:04:02,416
It did what it was made to do.

62
00:04:02,503 --> 00:04:04,287
But I cannot look behind.

63
00:04:04,374 --> 00:04:06,985
As any craftsman
worth his salt, I pray

64
00:04:07,072 --> 00:04:09,597
that my greatest
work lies ahead.

65
00:04:09,684 --> 00:04:11,773
Perhaps it does.

66
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,820
So home, then. Ithaca.
To your wife.

67
00:04:15,907 --> 00:04:17,213
Your city.

68
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:19,694
Yes, the only
journey worth the fight.

69
00:04:19,781 --> 00:04:21,609
Are you coming with me, Athena?

70
00:04:21,696 --> 00:04:24,002
Or is there another
reason for this visit?

71
00:04:24,089 --> 00:04:26,657
I come to bring you a warning.

72
00:04:26,744 --> 00:04:30,792
The gods,
they are fickle, Odysseus.

73
00:04:30,879 --> 00:04:34,665
They will not make your
journey home an easy one.

74
00:04:34,752 --> 00:04:38,234
Your trials will be
their entertainment.

75
00:04:38,321 --> 00:04:41,063
They know now that
you are a clever man.

76
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:43,152
They are jealous.

77
00:04:43,239 --> 00:04:46,634
They will use this against
you to show their power.

78
00:04:48,549 --> 00:04:51,029
Curse me or bless me,
Goddess on high.

79
00:04:51,116 --> 00:04:53,336
But be quick about it.

80
00:04:53,423 --> 00:04:55,599
In due time.

81
00:04:55,686 --> 00:04:58,123
And so begins your odyssey.

82
00:04:58,210 --> 00:05:00,517
And the game of the gods.

83
00:05:02,563 --> 00:05:04,434
Then pray for me, goddess.

84
00:05:04,521 --> 00:05:06,828
For I must reach home.

85
00:05:06,915 --> 00:05:08,612
For my kingdom.

86
00:05:08,699 --> 00:05:09,657
My everything.

87
00:05:09,744 --> 00:05:12,790
My son. My wife.

88
00:05:12,877 --> 00:05:14,052
My world will be gone.

89
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:31,766
I am looking for men.

90
00:05:31,853 --> 00:05:33,768
A crew for a dangerous journey.

91
00:05:35,726 --> 00:05:37,946
One the gods themselves
may turn their will against.

92
00:05:41,732 --> 00:05:45,170
Once I am home,
as king of Ithaca,

93
00:05:45,257 --> 00:05:49,914
I assure you each will
receive a suitable reward.

94
00:05:51,829 --> 00:05:55,006
You will never want for anything
for the rest of your lives.

95
00:05:56,704 --> 00:05:58,183
You have the word
of the king himself.

96
00:06:01,926 --> 00:06:04,451
A generous offer and reward.

97
00:06:04,538 --> 00:06:05,713
But are you as
good as your word?

98
00:06:07,715 --> 00:06:09,064
Polites!

99
00:06:09,151 --> 00:06:10,979
You look a little
bit past your prime.

100
00:06:11,066 --> 00:06:13,242
- By the gods!
- Old man.

101
00:06:13,329 --> 00:06:15,113
After all this time.

102
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:16,985
All this time and you still
haven’t gotten any better

103
00:06:17,072 --> 00:06:18,247
at motivational speeches.

104
00:06:19,770 --> 00:06:23,426
Absolute crickets
from you as well.

105
00:06:23,513 --> 00:06:25,950
This is a man who has saved
my life on numerous occasions.

106
00:06:26,037 --> 00:06:29,606
A debt we have both repaid
each other more than once

107
00:06:29,693 --> 00:06:32,566
in a war that has lasted
more than five years.

108
00:06:36,396 --> 00:06:37,701
Although it’s closer to ten now.

109
00:06:42,576 --> 00:06:44,142
But these days,

110
00:06:44,229 --> 00:06:47,885
I am much better collecting
scars than I am glory.

111
00:06:47,972 --> 00:06:50,888
But, you will find plenty
of much younger,

112
00:06:50,975 --> 00:06:53,630
probably not as bright men
more than willing to join you.

113
00:06:53,717 --> 00:06:55,458
But probably not here,

114
00:06:55,545 --> 00:06:57,547
sipping goblets in
the middle of the day.

115
00:06:57,634 --> 00:06:59,027
And the rest here,

116
00:06:59,114 --> 00:07:00,637
well, they know not
the true cost of war,

117
00:07:00,724 --> 00:07:03,161
though they may be eager.

118
00:07:03,248 --> 00:07:05,294
- As once we were.
- As we once were.

119
00:07:05,381 --> 00:07:08,645
Listening to our fathers
and our grandfathers stories.

120
00:07:08,732 --> 00:07:11,692
And just more than
willing to prove ourselves.

121
00:07:11,779 --> 00:07:13,433
Can I trust them?

122
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:14,912
About as much as
you can trust me.

123
00:07:16,871 --> 00:07:19,308
All I know is that what’s
waiting for you back in Ithaca,

124
00:07:19,395 --> 00:07:20,744
now that is your reward.

125
00:07:20,831 --> 00:07:22,267
And you deserve it, too.

126
00:07:22,354 --> 00:07:23,355
But for the rest of us...

127
00:07:26,707 --> 00:07:30,145
Join me.
I need men who I can trust.

128
00:07:30,232 --> 00:07:32,364
Men with
experience in battle.

129
00:07:32,452 --> 00:07:33,975
No, no, no. I will not be
going to any battle.

130
00:07:34,062 --> 00:07:35,803
Or any wars.
Ever again.

131
00:07:35,890 --> 00:07:37,979
I am unpersuadable.
I cannot be persuaded.

132
00:07:38,066 --> 00:07:40,851
Did I mention the gold?

133
00:07:40,938 --> 00:07:43,375
Well, did I mention
unpersuaded?

134
00:07:43,463 --> 00:07:44,420
When do we start?

135
00:07:44,507 --> 00:07:45,508
As soon as we recruit two more.

136
00:07:45,595 --> 00:07:46,814
- Two more?
- Yes.

137
00:07:46,901 --> 00:07:48,250
Okay. Let’s go.

138
00:07:50,208 --> 00:07:52,036
Alkimos the Navigator.

139
00:07:52,123 --> 00:07:55,387
From what I heard, he was
the one that saved a ship

140
00:07:55,475 --> 00:07:57,128
of fishermen from a cyclone.

141
00:07:57,215 --> 00:07:58,826
Really?

142
00:07:58,913 --> 00:08:01,785
I’ve heard of you,
King Odysseus.

143
00:08:01,872 --> 00:08:03,831
What have you heard?

144
00:08:03,918 --> 00:08:06,964
That you, sacker of cities,
son of Laertes,

145
00:08:07,051 --> 00:08:09,445
are favored by the gods.

146
00:08:09,532 --> 00:08:13,580
Not all the gods.
One or two. Maybe less.

147
00:08:13,667 --> 00:08:15,364
You survived the Trojan War.

148
00:08:15,451 --> 00:08:17,148
You’re a king.

149
00:08:17,235 --> 00:08:19,281
The King of Ithaca.

150
00:08:19,368 --> 00:08:21,283
I have a family
that comes from Ithaca.

151
00:08:21,370 --> 00:08:23,764
I haven’t seen them
since I was little.

152
00:08:23,851 --> 00:08:24,765
You know, my king--

153
00:08:24,852 --> 00:08:26,070
I’m not king today.

154
00:08:29,073 --> 00:08:30,466
But if you help me
get home, I will be.

155
00:08:35,253 --> 00:08:37,604
I’m a good navigator.

156
00:08:37,691 --> 00:08:39,388
But you mentioned gold.

157
00:08:39,475 --> 00:08:40,781
Did I?

158
00:08:40,868 --> 00:08:43,218
Oh. You did. Yes.
You mentioned gold.

159
00:08:43,305 --> 00:08:44,828
He mentioned gold.

160
00:08:44,915 --> 00:08:45,916
Hmm.

161
00:08:46,003 --> 00:08:48,005
I don’t seek gold.

162
00:08:48,092 --> 00:08:49,877
I’m bored.

163
00:08:49,964 --> 00:08:52,053
Bored of fishing
boats filled with old men

164
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:54,316
with putrid smelling cargo.

165
00:08:56,666 --> 00:08:57,798
I seek adventure.

166
00:08:57,885 --> 00:08:59,451
New shores.

167
00:08:59,539 --> 00:09:01,671
Like in the stories.

168
00:09:01,758 --> 00:09:02,803
Stories about you.

169
00:09:07,938 --> 00:09:09,679
Then join me,

170
00:09:09,766 --> 00:09:12,856
and we will make stories
that they will never forget.

171
00:09:12,943 --> 00:09:16,904
And I promise you, it will
be anything but boring.

172
00:09:16,991 --> 00:09:20,037
And once we reach Ithaca,
you can join my guard

173
00:09:20,124 --> 00:09:23,214
and help me protect
our fair kingdom.

174
00:09:23,301 --> 00:09:24,215
My king.

175
00:09:26,261 --> 00:09:28,176
Now, how are you at fighting?

176
00:09:28,263 --> 00:09:29,699
I can handle my own.

177
00:09:29,786 --> 00:09:31,614
But if you want a
force to be reckoned with,

178
00:09:31,701 --> 00:09:33,660
you want to
recruit Eurylochus,

179
00:09:33,747 --> 00:09:35,618
I’ve never seen another
man fight quite like he has.

180
00:09:38,621 --> 00:09:40,928
We’ll pay him a visit next.

181
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:46,629
So what say you, Eurylochus?

182
00:09:46,716 --> 00:09:49,632
I have killed a lot of
men to protect my home.

183
00:09:49,719 --> 00:09:51,939
War has plagued our
country for many years.

184
00:09:55,986 --> 00:09:57,858
How are you at sea?

185
00:09:57,945 --> 00:10:00,861
I’m a fast swimmer, and
even faster with a sword.

186
00:10:00,948 --> 00:10:02,471
Hm. Is that so?

187
00:10:02,558 --> 00:10:03,646
Hm.

188
00:10:05,169 --> 00:10:06,649
But now that
the war is over,

189
00:10:06,736 --> 00:10:09,086
I’ve found work at a
vineyard up the road.

190
00:10:09,173 --> 00:10:11,219
The owner, he treats me well.

191
00:10:11,306 --> 00:10:16,050
And his daughter,
Talia, she’s...

192
00:10:16,137 --> 00:10:20,010
I just don’t know if I can
commit to a journey this long.

193
00:10:20,097 --> 00:10:24,058
You will be in Ithaca
before sundown two days hence.

194
00:10:24,145 --> 00:10:26,060
We have to be.

195
00:10:26,147 --> 00:10:28,410
And if you decide to
stay in our fair kingdom,

196
00:10:28,497 --> 00:10:31,413
you will live a life
of ease in Ithaca.

197
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,850
And once there,
she can join you.

198
00:10:36,723 --> 00:10:38,507
If you teach me
how to be as fast

199
00:10:38,594 --> 00:10:40,596
as you, my king
Odysseus, then I’m in.

200
00:10:44,426 --> 00:10:46,515
And as I promised...

201
00:10:46,602 --> 00:10:48,082
We need gold.

202
00:10:48,169 --> 00:10:50,693
You said we need to
be to Ithaca in two days.

203
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:53,957
Yes. I have a plan.

204
00:10:54,044 --> 00:10:55,219
But you must trust me.

205
00:10:56,917 --> 00:10:58,222
I’m with you to
the end, my king.

206
00:11:00,355 --> 00:11:01,835
Or thereabouts.

207
00:11:01,922 --> 00:11:05,490
From Troy, Odysseus
<i>launched his journey home</i>

208
00:11:05,577 --> 00:11:08,885
<i>with 12 ships and 600 men.</i>

209
00:11:08,972 --> 00:11:13,194
<i>Back in Ithaca,</i>
<i>Odysseus’s dear wife Penelope</i>

210
00:11:13,281 --> 00:11:17,154
<i>dreams of her love</i>
<i>bearing the brutal seas</i>

211
00:11:17,241 --> 00:11:19,679
<i>to find his way home to her.</i>

212
00:11:46,488 --> 00:11:47,532
Odysseus?

213
00:12:06,726 --> 00:12:07,727
God’s above.

214
00:12:10,164 --> 00:12:12,340
You’ve shown me Odysseus lives.

215
00:12:14,472 --> 00:12:15,735
Now bring him home.

216
00:12:17,824 --> 00:12:20,740
Bring him home now,
or these lips

217
00:12:20,827 --> 00:12:23,743
shall never again
speak your names.

218
00:12:42,196 --> 00:12:44,198
A growing boy needs food.

219
00:12:44,285 --> 00:12:47,157
A boy needs many things,
mother, but I am not a boy.

220
00:12:47,244 --> 00:12:48,550
Not any...

221
00:12:50,987 --> 00:12:51,945
Mother.

222
00:12:52,032 --> 00:12:54,208
I’ve seen him, Telemachus.

223
00:12:54,295 --> 00:12:55,426
I’ve seen your father.

224
00:12:55,513 --> 00:12:57,037
You’re bleeding.
You must sit.

225
00:12:57,124 --> 00:12:59,735
No, my son.
We must stand and fight.

226
00:12:59,822 --> 00:13:01,171
What have you seen?

227
00:13:02,782 --> 00:13:03,695
A vision.

228
00:13:07,351 --> 00:13:08,265
A message from the gods.

229
00:13:11,094 --> 00:13:13,009
Troy burns.

230
00:13:13,096 --> 00:13:15,620
Your father, he is triumphant.

231
00:13:15,707 --> 00:13:18,493
He is coming home, but it
may not be fast enough.

232
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:21,583
Ithaca is vulnerable now.

233
00:13:21,670 --> 00:13:23,237
Now that the war is over,

234
00:13:23,324 --> 00:13:26,370
Odysseus must return to
reclaim the throne, otherwise

235
00:13:26,457 --> 00:13:29,765
another can claim this
kingdom as his own.

236
00:13:29,852 --> 00:13:33,813
Your father would rather
die than let that happen.

237
00:13:33,900 --> 00:13:35,553
He will return.
He must.

238
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:38,600
And in his absence,
I am the man of this house,

239
00:13:38,687 --> 00:13:40,950
and I say that we...

240
00:13:41,037 --> 00:13:43,518
You will be a man
soon enough, my love. You will.

241
00:13:43,605 --> 00:13:47,478
But you cannot be king.
Not yet.

242
00:13:48,915 --> 00:13:50,612
And therein lies our fight.

243
00:13:52,657 --> 00:13:56,400
Each day that your father
is away, others become

244
00:13:56,487 --> 00:13:59,664
more willing to claim
this kingdom as their own.

245
00:13:59,751 --> 00:14:04,887
If he does not return,
I will not also lose you.

246
00:14:04,974 --> 00:14:07,672
So we must be strong, my son.

247
00:14:07,759 --> 00:14:10,850
Together, we must
be like your father.

248
00:14:12,025 --> 00:14:14,244
Smart and resourceful.

249
00:14:14,331 --> 00:14:17,682
And protect this
kingdom without violence.

250
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:20,424
Clever.

251
00:14:25,473 --> 00:14:28,302
So clever as to make
the gods jealous.

252
00:14:30,652 --> 00:14:33,655
Whether we want it or not,
the heavens will continue to

253
00:14:33,742 --> 00:14:36,527
move without Odysseus here
by our side where he belongs.

254
00:14:39,052 --> 00:14:40,967
If he does not return soon

255
00:14:41,054 --> 00:14:43,839
after victory
has been declared,

256
00:14:43,926 --> 00:14:46,146
then by law he will have
abandoned his duties as king.

257
00:14:48,322 --> 00:14:52,065
And a suitor, any suitor,

258
00:14:52,152 --> 00:14:54,894
may lay claim to this house,

259
00:14:54,981 --> 00:14:56,591
and to the crown of Ithaca.

260
00:14:56,678 --> 00:14:58,158
And to you.

261
00:15:00,290 --> 00:15:03,511
The law only reaches so far.

262
00:15:03,598 --> 00:15:08,342
When they come,
and come they will,

263
00:15:08,429 --> 00:15:10,257
we must be ready.

264
00:15:10,344 --> 00:15:15,523
And I am ready to fight for you
and for father and the kingdom.

265
00:15:15,610 --> 00:15:19,135
Son, this is not going
to be a sword fight.

266
00:15:19,222 --> 00:15:21,921
Or even a fight of fists.

267
00:15:22,008 --> 00:15:24,619
This will be a fight
of words and propriety.

268
00:15:27,100 --> 00:15:29,450
We need to buy him time.

269
00:15:29,537 --> 00:15:33,280
Keep the suitors busy.

270
00:15:33,367 --> 00:15:35,978
Their bellies full
and distracted.

271
00:15:37,632 --> 00:15:41,723
Now come and honor me
like you do him.

272
00:15:41,810 --> 00:15:44,813
The war is over now.

273
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:46,641
But our battle
is just beginning.

274
00:15:59,262 --> 00:16:00,829
Goddess Athena...

275
00:16:02,657 --> 00:16:04,528
Help us.

276
00:16:04,615 --> 00:16:06,269
I pray.

277
00:16:06,356 --> 00:16:08,880
But help Odysseus.

278
00:16:08,968 --> 00:16:10,099
Help my father.

279
00:16:11,971 --> 00:16:13,189
Please.

280
00:16:13,276 --> 00:16:15,931
The mighty storm
<i>that struck Odysseus</i>

281
00:16:16,018 --> 00:16:21,197
<i>ravaged the sea for ten days,</i>
<i>taking them far off course.</i>

282
00:16:21,284 --> 00:16:23,808
<i>After battling the sea,</i>

283
00:16:23,895 --> 00:16:27,029
<i>Odysseus has arrived</i>
<i>at his home in Ithaca...</i>

284
00:16:27,116 --> 00:16:29,162
<i>or so he thought.</i>

285
00:16:29,249 --> 00:16:30,554
This is not Ithaca, Athena!

286
00:16:33,166 --> 00:16:35,995
This is another one
of the gods’ cruel jokes.

287
00:16:39,824 --> 00:16:40,738
I must get home.

288
00:16:44,264 --> 00:16:45,613
I will get home.

289
00:16:52,576 --> 00:16:53,447
Penelope.

290
00:16:55,623 --> 00:16:56,667
Sweet Penelope.

291
00:16:59,105 --> 00:17:00,454
I will come home.

292
00:17:04,893 --> 00:17:06,895
Look at you keeping
step with the gods.

293
00:17:10,377 --> 00:17:11,595
Athena.

294
00:17:11,682 --> 00:17:13,945
I am many leagues off course.

295
00:17:14,033 --> 00:17:17,384
I’m only two days
journey from Ithaca.

296
00:17:17,471 --> 00:17:19,299
Please.

297
00:17:19,386 --> 00:17:22,737
Spirit me home to Ithaca,
great goddess.

298
00:17:22,824 --> 00:17:24,913
You know that I cannot.

299
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,090
It is not within
my power to do so.

300
00:17:28,177 --> 00:17:30,484
But think, Odysseus.

301
00:17:30,571 --> 00:17:33,965
Perhaps there is a reason
that you are not yet home.

302
00:17:34,053 --> 00:17:37,012
The gods have another plan.

303
00:17:37,099 --> 00:17:38,013
I see.

304
00:17:41,147 --> 00:17:43,497
I am here to help.

305
00:17:43,584 --> 00:17:46,978
I can offer you help three
times along your journey.

306
00:17:47,066 --> 00:17:49,198
Three gifts.

307
00:17:49,285 --> 00:17:50,678
Only three?

308
00:17:50,765 --> 00:17:52,810
’Tis all I am permitted.

309
00:17:52,897 --> 00:17:55,378
I am helping because
I see myself

310
00:17:55,465 --> 00:17:58,990
in your clever mind and
your tactician’s brain.

311
00:17:59,078 --> 00:18:02,559
Your glory in
the Trojan War honors me.

312
00:18:02,646 --> 00:18:05,519
And that is why I will help you.

313
00:18:05,606 --> 00:18:06,955
I am flattered, goddess.

314
00:18:08,739 --> 00:18:09,653
I must decline.

315
00:18:11,612 --> 00:18:13,353
I am offended.

316
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:17,357
Odysseus.
Refusing a gift from the gods?

317
00:18:17,444 --> 00:18:19,402
From Athena herself.

318
00:18:19,489 --> 00:18:22,840
If you cannot spirit
me home then please

319
00:18:22,927 --> 00:18:28,629
understand I must ask for
help only when I need it.

320
00:18:28,716 --> 00:18:33,329
When all hope is lost, my wits
abandon me and death beckons.

321
00:18:35,592 --> 00:18:38,813
In this moment I will
honor your generosity

322
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:41,294
for I know the
value of godly gifts.

323
00:18:43,426 --> 00:18:46,516
Then you must know even now

324
00:18:46,603 --> 00:18:49,650
dogs are sniffing
at your house

325
00:18:49,737 --> 00:18:51,260
and at your wife.

326
00:18:53,001 --> 00:18:56,265
The gods are testing
your cleverness, Odysseus.

327
00:18:59,312 --> 00:19:01,923
Then I pray the
gods do not stall me

328
00:19:02,010 --> 00:19:03,664
any longer than
they already have.

329
00:19:06,362 --> 00:19:08,408
Go, hero.

330
00:19:08,495 --> 00:19:10,497
Honor me and prove your worth.

331
00:19:11,672 --> 00:19:13,413
Get home.

332
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:16,720
No matter what horrors the
gods place upon your path.

333
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:27,078
The opportunity here
is too great to pass up.

334
00:19:27,166 --> 00:19:30,343
A kingdom, a crown,

335
00:19:30,430 --> 00:19:33,215
and even the queen herself

336
00:19:33,302 --> 00:19:35,870
all free for the taking.

337
00:19:35,957 --> 00:19:39,743
And it should be easy
enough owing to propriety

338
00:19:39,830 --> 00:19:42,311
and Grecian law.

339
00:19:42,398 --> 00:19:47,142
All suitors
must be taken seriously.

340
00:19:47,229 --> 00:19:48,361
And I will be the first.

341
00:19:48,970 --> 00:19:50,058
Ha!

342
00:19:51,451 --> 00:19:54,671
The early bird gets the kingdom.

343
00:19:57,065 --> 00:20:01,243
She’ll have no choice
but to honor my demands.

344
00:20:01,330 --> 00:20:02,636
All of my demands.

345
00:20:15,344 --> 00:20:18,869
Antinous just said
the most awful things.

346
00:20:18,956 --> 00:20:22,133
Threatening to take the crown,
our house, and you!

347
00:20:24,179 --> 00:20:25,615
Then it has begun.

348
00:20:25,702 --> 00:20:28,923
We must be on our
guard, Telemachus.

349
00:20:29,010 --> 00:20:31,404
Always polite and inviting.

350
00:20:33,754 --> 00:20:34,929
But on our guard.

351
00:20:38,976 --> 00:20:41,457
But on our guard!

352
00:20:41,544 --> 00:20:44,982
Perilous winds
<i>and unsettling new lands</i>

353
00:20:45,069 --> 00:20:47,898
<i> </i><i>take their toll on the voyagers</i>

354
00:20:47,985 --> 00:20:52,947
<i>who now explore</i>
<i>a small island, rich in gold.</i>

355
00:20:53,034 --> 00:20:55,341
Is this part of
the plan, Lord Odysseus?

356
00:20:55,428 --> 00:20:56,690
Indeed, Eurylochus.

357
00:20:56,777 --> 00:20:59,258
Now stay close,
and your lips closed.

358
00:21:01,129 --> 00:21:04,219
I don’t quite understand
what this has to do with gold.

359
00:21:04,306 --> 00:21:06,047
We’re going to retrieve gold.

360
00:21:06,134 --> 00:21:10,878
My gold that was
stolen by a cyclops.

361
00:21:10,965 --> 00:21:14,490
He’s the son of Poseidon,
God of the sea.

362
00:21:14,577 --> 00:21:16,187
A real cyclops?

363
00:21:16,275 --> 00:21:17,537
Why didn’t you tell us sooner?

364
00:21:17,624 --> 00:21:18,842
They eat people, so I’ve heard.

365
00:21:18,929 --> 00:21:21,192
My brave crew...

366
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:22,933
Alkimos, you wanted adventure.

367
00:21:23,020 --> 00:21:24,805
Here it is.

368
00:21:24,892 --> 00:21:29,418
Eurylochus, you wanted to stay
alive in a cave with a Cyclops.

369
00:21:29,505 --> 00:21:30,985
You must learn to be faster.

370
00:21:31,072 --> 00:21:33,379
And Polites,
now you wanted gold.

371
00:21:36,295 --> 00:21:39,210
Yes, this cyclops has
been hoarding gold for years.

372
00:21:39,298 --> 00:21:41,169
Mine and others.

373
00:21:41,256 --> 00:21:44,172
And that gold will
help me pay your rewards.

374
00:21:44,259 --> 00:21:47,784
He’s been terrorizing this
mountainside for many years.

375
00:21:47,871 --> 00:21:49,046
It’s time we return the favor.

376
00:21:51,353 --> 00:21:55,618
Today, our journey
begins with a cyclops.

377
00:21:55,705 --> 00:21:58,229
Or it ends before it begins.

378
00:21:58,317 --> 00:22:00,144
Now who’s with me?

379
00:22:00,231 --> 00:22:01,363
My king.

380
00:22:01,450 --> 00:22:02,930
Help! Help!

381
00:22:04,410 --> 00:22:05,933
Help! Help!

382
00:22:19,250 --> 00:22:20,861
He... He ate him.

383
00:22:22,558 --> 00:22:27,258
Do as I say when I say,
then we will win the day.

384
00:22:27,346 --> 00:22:28,738
All right, what’s the plan?

385
00:22:28,825 --> 00:22:30,523
We’ll circle the
base of the mountain.

386
00:22:30,610 --> 00:22:32,394
Go inside.

387
00:22:32,481 --> 00:22:35,179
Stay close, and downwind.

388
00:22:35,266 --> 00:22:38,269
Cyclops, he can smell
us nearly a league away.

389
00:22:39,967 --> 00:22:40,837
Hold, hold, hold.

390
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:47,670
At Troy, during the
war, you were there?

391
00:22:47,757 --> 00:22:50,064
I sure was.

392
00:22:50,151 --> 00:22:51,413
I barely survived,
and I probably wouldn’t have

393
00:22:51,500 --> 00:22:53,589
if it weren’t for Odysseus.

394
00:22:53,676 --> 00:22:56,549
Saved me from
an entire garrison.

395
00:22:56,636 --> 00:22:58,507
Picked them off one by one.

396
00:22:58,594 --> 00:23:01,205
Hiding in the shadows,
which is a pretty big

397
00:23:01,292 --> 00:23:04,034
feat for him, considering
he’s a big bastard.

398
00:23:04,121 --> 00:23:06,210
I’ve only heard stories.

399
00:23:06,297 --> 00:23:07,647
Legends, really.

400
00:23:07,734 --> 00:23:10,650
Well, not everything
you hear is true.

401
00:23:10,737 --> 00:23:13,261
Only half of what
I just said was true.

402
00:23:13,348 --> 00:23:15,089
Is he as good a man as they say?

403
00:23:15,176 --> 00:23:17,483
He’s a great man, though
he’d never admit it.

404
00:23:17,570 --> 00:23:19,659
But out of humility.

405
00:23:19,746 --> 00:23:22,923
He’s a man who
values many virtues.

406
00:23:23,010 --> 00:23:27,710
But the one he values above
all others is cleverness.

407
00:23:27,797 --> 00:23:33,368
You see, a good man engenders
himself to friends, family.

408
00:23:33,455 --> 00:23:37,764
A clever man,
a clever man survives.

409
00:23:37,851 --> 00:23:38,721
Quickly, now.

410
00:23:41,985 --> 00:23:45,293
Gods willing, we will, too.

411
00:23:56,913 --> 00:24:00,221
Keep your eyes open and
your wits about you, man.

412
00:24:00,308 --> 00:24:03,659
Odysseus, you know
no-ones actually entered

413
00:24:03,746 --> 00:24:07,402
the Cyclops cave and
lived to tell the tale.

414
00:24:07,489 --> 00:24:08,577
Not until today they don’t.

415
00:24:13,408 --> 00:24:16,019
He will be busy
with his guest.

416
00:24:17,281 --> 00:24:19,588
This is our chance.

417
00:24:19,675 --> 00:24:21,938
We sneak in,
find the treasure,

418
00:24:22,025 --> 00:24:24,854
take what we can carry,
stay quiet,

419
00:24:24,941 --> 00:24:28,031
get out the same way.

420
00:24:28,118 --> 00:24:29,946
Stay with me.

421
00:24:30,033 --> 00:24:30,947
Stay low.

422
00:24:39,434 --> 00:24:40,827
But how do we find the treasure?

423
00:24:42,959 --> 00:24:45,092
Where does the cyclops
even keep its treasure?

424
00:24:47,311 --> 00:24:50,924
It smells like death.

425
00:24:51,011 --> 00:24:53,404
What better place to
hide your gold than in

426
00:24:53,492 --> 00:24:58,192
a place that smells so bad
that no one dares enter?

427
00:24:58,279 --> 00:25:00,934
Use your nose.
Follow its direction.

428
00:25:01,021 --> 00:25:05,982
Is... Is somebody here?

429
00:25:06,069 --> 00:25:09,333
I smell...
I smell men.

430
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:13,033
I smell your fear.

431
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:17,124
You can’t...
You can’t hide for long

432
00:25:17,211 --> 00:25:21,694
and Polyphemus,
the son of Poseidon,

433
00:25:21,781 --> 00:25:25,393
is still hungry.

434
00:25:27,700 --> 00:25:29,049
There.

435
00:25:29,136 --> 00:25:30,964
The smell of the
worst is that way.

436
00:25:31,051 --> 00:25:32,095
Move, move.

437
00:25:49,417 --> 00:25:50,505
Let’s move.

438
00:26:03,257 --> 00:26:04,432
Quiet.

439
00:26:12,005 --> 00:26:13,180
Holy gold.

440
00:26:14,268 --> 00:26:15,835
Reward.

441
00:26:15,922 --> 00:26:17,445
Rewards for good men.

442
00:26:17,532 --> 00:26:19,229
Shh.

443
00:26:19,316 --> 00:26:20,491
Okay, quickly.

444
00:26:20,579 --> 00:26:22,276
Take everything you can.

445
00:26:22,363 --> 00:26:25,845
Actually, only what you can
carry, just like Odysseus said.

446
00:26:25,932 --> 00:26:27,847
Wait, where is Odysseus?

447
00:26:27,934 --> 00:26:30,501
Odysseus is gone.

448
00:26:30,589 --> 00:26:32,808
You’ve come for my gold!

449
00:26:32,895 --> 00:26:34,244
Shh!

450
00:26:34,331 --> 00:26:37,508
- Thieves!
- Okay, take cover!

451
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:44,037
Thieves, who are you?

452
00:26:46,387 --> 00:26:47,736
We’re nobody!

453
00:26:50,347 --> 00:26:52,175
Grab the treasure! Run!

454
00:27:02,098 --> 00:27:03,752
Go! I’ll keep him busy!

455
00:27:05,798 --> 00:27:08,931
We fight with you,
my king!

456
00:27:14,676 --> 00:27:15,634
Eurylochus!

457
00:27:16,765 --> 00:27:17,897
Odysseus!

458
00:27:17,984 --> 00:27:19,202
Polites, help! Ah!

459
00:27:21,988 --> 00:27:24,730
No! No!

460
00:27:24,817 --> 00:27:27,341
You will die, foul beast!

461
00:27:27,428 --> 00:27:28,995
For Eurylochus!

462
00:27:32,694 --> 00:27:34,348
Nobody?

463
00:27:34,435 --> 00:27:35,741
It calls for help.

464
00:27:37,177 --> 00:27:39,135
No one will come!

465
00:27:39,222 --> 00:27:41,572
It calls out for nobody.

466
00:27:41,660 --> 00:27:42,573
No one will come.

467
00:27:47,666 --> 00:27:51,060
May the gods bless
you, Eurylochus,

468
00:27:51,147 --> 00:27:53,367
and speed you
through the underworld

469
00:27:53,454 --> 00:27:56,979
as quickly as
the gods will permit.

470
00:27:57,066 --> 00:27:59,939
Now, go.
Grab the treasure.

471
00:28:00,026 --> 00:28:02,898
The son of Poseidon will
not lick his wounds long.

472
00:28:02,985 --> 00:28:04,465
Go!

473
00:28:04,552 --> 00:28:06,815
- Only take what we need.
- Only take what we need.

474
00:28:06,902 --> 00:28:08,730
Yes, enough for a boat.

475
00:28:08,817 --> 00:28:10,514
Provisions and no more.

476
00:28:10,601 --> 00:28:12,255
Okay, here’s a bit of gold.
There’s a bit of this...

477
00:28:12,342 --> 00:28:13,866
- Hurry!
- Yes, grab that.

478
00:28:13,953 --> 00:28:16,999
That’s expensive, I like that.
Okay, and...

479
00:28:17,086 --> 00:28:18,044
Yes, let’s go.

480
00:28:18,131 --> 00:28:19,828
Go, go!

481
00:28:31,884 --> 00:28:33,276
Charie!

482
00:28:33,363 --> 00:28:36,410
And greetings to the
great house of Odysseus.

483
00:28:36,497 --> 00:28:38,673
Greetings to you, traveler.

484
00:28:38,760 --> 00:28:41,458
Your voice seems so...

485
00:28:41,545 --> 00:28:43,243
familiar.

486
00:28:43,330 --> 00:28:45,680
But our house is
in no condition...

487
00:28:45,767 --> 00:28:48,422
Aye, aye.
Well met, my lord Telelachus.

488
00:28:48,509 --> 00:28:49,858
Telemachus.

489
00:28:51,642 --> 00:28:54,558
Apologies. Apologies.

490
00:28:54,645 --> 00:28:56,952
I am Antinous.

491
00:28:57,039 --> 00:29:00,956
And I come bearing gifts.

492
00:29:01,043 --> 00:29:04,003
The gift of my protection.

493
00:29:06,092 --> 00:29:08,094
A gracious home.

494
00:29:08,181 --> 00:29:09,965
A kingly estate, indeed.

495
00:29:17,538 --> 00:29:20,671
Ready our ship, men.
We leave port immediately.

496
00:29:20,759 --> 00:29:21,716
- My king.
- My king.

497
00:29:39,603 --> 00:29:41,780
Come to wish us farewell?

498
00:29:41,867 --> 00:29:43,042
I have.

499
00:29:43,129 --> 00:29:46,045
And I come bearing a gift.

500
00:29:46,132 --> 00:29:49,962
A bag of wind
to speed you along your journey.

501
00:29:50,049 --> 00:29:52,007
Perhaps if you’d
accepted my offer of help,

502
00:29:52,094 --> 00:29:54,836
and asked for stronger
and faster ships,

503
00:29:54,923 --> 00:29:56,620
you would not need it.

504
00:29:56,707 --> 00:29:59,623
Or you would already
be closer to home.

505
00:29:59,710 --> 00:30:01,103
My ship.

506
00:30:01,190 --> 00:30:03,627
I would not trust
a boat from the gods.

507
00:30:03,714 --> 00:30:08,894
Who knows if the previous owners
seek revenge and come find me?

508
00:30:08,981 --> 00:30:11,200
And what of the previous
owners of this treasure?

509
00:30:13,289 --> 00:30:15,204
My ship.

510
00:30:15,291 --> 00:30:16,292
My treasure.

511
00:30:16,379 --> 00:30:17,511
My men.

512
00:30:22,733 --> 00:30:23,952
What’s left of them.

513
00:30:32,569 --> 00:30:33,919
By Olympus.

514
00:30:36,660 --> 00:30:38,227
One of the gods
speaking to Odysseus.

515
00:30:40,708 --> 00:30:41,840
Gifts.

516
00:30:41,927 --> 00:30:43,406
Gifts from the gods themselves.

517
00:30:46,540 --> 00:30:48,847
It’s a generous gift.

518
00:30:48,934 --> 00:30:51,066
And I accept your bag of wind.

519
00:30:51,153 --> 00:30:54,809
But it is not one of your
three gifts until I use it.

520
00:30:54,896 --> 00:30:57,333
Why not use it now?

521
00:30:57,420 --> 00:30:58,900
I will use it.

522
00:30:58,987 --> 00:31:01,860
But I will use it when I must.

523
00:31:01,947 --> 00:31:04,775
The gifts of a god
come with a heavy price.

524
00:31:07,996 --> 00:31:11,260
Hurry home, son of Laertes.

525
00:31:11,347 --> 00:31:12,435
Honor me.

526
00:31:16,352 --> 00:31:17,266
Rewards.

527
00:31:24,621 --> 00:31:27,059
Odysseus!

528
00:31:27,146 --> 00:31:28,103
Was that...

529
00:31:28,190 --> 00:31:29,583
Athena herself.

530
00:31:29,670 --> 00:31:31,367
Come to wish us a farewell.

531
00:31:32,673 --> 00:31:34,022
Could she help us?

532
00:31:34,109 --> 00:31:36,459
Help us get there faster.

533
00:31:36,546 --> 00:31:37,896
I would give my life
many times over if it

534
00:31:37,983 --> 00:31:40,289
meant getting home
sooner to my loved ones.

535
00:31:40,376 --> 00:31:44,119
But the gods play
dangerous games with men.

536
00:31:44,206 --> 00:31:47,557
They could spirit us home
with just a mere thought.

537
00:31:47,644 --> 00:31:49,690
Or the breath from their lungs.

538
00:31:49,777 --> 00:31:52,954
Or they could smite us where we
stand and send us to our dooms.

539
00:31:53,041 --> 00:31:55,652
To them, it’s all the same.

540
00:31:55,739 --> 00:31:57,567
That’s why we
must tread lightly.

541
00:31:57,654 --> 00:32:00,744
Especially with the ones
who seem the most generous.

542
00:32:00,831 --> 00:32:02,398
And what of that?

543
00:32:02,485 --> 00:32:03,965
Is that a gift?
A reward?

544
00:32:04,052 --> 00:32:05,836
No, it’s nothing.

545
00:32:05,924 --> 00:32:07,490
It’s a good luck charm.

546
00:32:07,577 --> 00:32:09,579
Now, Polites, let’s go home.

547
00:32:20,634 --> 00:32:21,983
A guest in my home.

548
00:32:22,070 --> 00:32:24,768
Aye, but not just any guest.

549
00:32:24,855 --> 00:32:26,683
Antinous, my queen.

550
00:32:26,770 --> 00:32:28,642
At your service.

551
00:32:28,729 --> 00:32:30,513
Well met, Antinous.

552
00:32:30,600 --> 00:32:33,777
And to what do we
owe this generous visit?

553
00:32:33,864 --> 00:32:38,652
I come to pay my respects
to the great Odysseus himself.

554
00:32:38,739 --> 00:32:41,611
And to those he left behind.

555
00:32:41,698 --> 00:32:43,570
He will return. He...

556
00:32:43,657 --> 00:32:44,919
And we accept that respect

557
00:32:45,006 --> 00:32:46,573
in the spirit
that it was given.

558
00:32:49,228 --> 00:32:50,359
Thank you, Antinous.

559
00:32:52,448 --> 00:32:56,975
And I am here to confirm
that this home is safe.

560
00:32:57,062 --> 00:33:01,370
Safe from any who might think
to encroach upon its sanctity.

561
00:33:01,457 --> 00:33:04,373
And to offer my
allegiance and my duty to you

562
00:33:04,460 --> 00:33:06,941
and yours
and to this kingdom.

563
00:33:08,682 --> 00:33:12,381
And to offer my services
to you

564
00:33:12,468 --> 00:33:15,645
as husband and father...

565
00:33:18,866 --> 00:33:19,910
and king.

566
00:33:19,998 --> 00:33:21,303
There is no need for that.

567
00:33:21,390 --> 00:33:22,304
Or for you. My father--

568
00:33:22,391 --> 00:33:24,306
You’ll forgive my son.

569
00:33:24,393 --> 00:33:27,048
He can be rather spirited.

570
00:33:31,835 --> 00:33:33,011
Much like his father.

571
00:33:34,621 --> 00:33:37,450
A generous proposal, indeed.

572
00:33:37,537 --> 00:33:40,714
But we have only just met.

573
00:33:40,801 --> 00:33:42,803
Perhaps we can get
better acquainted first.

574
00:33:44,761 --> 00:33:47,112
A fair suggestion.

575
00:33:47,199 --> 00:33:48,548
Right and good.

576
00:33:48,635 --> 00:33:52,117
But I have to admit,
my gracious queen.

577
00:33:52,204 --> 00:33:54,684
I am famished.

578
00:33:54,771 --> 00:34:00,038
And my lips are parched from
my long journey to your door.

579
00:34:00,125 --> 00:34:03,998
Yes. Refreshments might
do us all some good.

580
00:34:07,045 --> 00:34:08,002
Telemachus.

581
00:34:09,525 --> 00:34:10,744
I could use your help.

582
00:34:15,096 --> 00:34:16,054
Take your time.

583
00:34:17,403 --> 00:34:18,708
Take your time.

584
00:34:20,406 --> 00:34:21,711
I’m in no rush.

585
00:34:24,975 --> 00:34:26,542
Mother, what are you doing?

586
00:34:26,629 --> 00:34:28,501
My duty, my son.

587
00:34:28,588 --> 00:34:31,765
And I expect you to do the same.

588
00:34:31,852 --> 00:34:33,114
Antinous is our guest.

589
00:34:33,201 --> 00:34:35,029
Antinous is a
lecherous fool who...

590
00:34:38,902 --> 00:34:40,121
Mother, I--

591
00:34:40,208 --> 00:34:43,429
There will be
other fools, son.

592
00:34:43,516 --> 00:34:44,952
He is merely the first.

593
00:34:45,039 --> 00:34:47,302
It’s just like I warned you.

594
00:34:47,389 --> 00:34:50,436
Every day Ithaca
goes without a king

595
00:34:50,523 --> 00:34:53,874
and our house without
our dearest love,

596
00:34:53,961 --> 00:34:55,658
we edge closer
and closer to chaos.

597
00:34:58,226 --> 00:35:01,055
While he is here, the others in

598
00:35:01,142 --> 00:35:03,231
waiting will keep
their distance.

599
00:35:06,408 --> 00:35:08,671
Concerned about their chances,
making assumptions...

600
00:35:10,847 --> 00:35:13,023
Peace is kept.

601
00:35:13,111 --> 00:35:14,547
But mother, I don’t think...

602
00:35:14,634 --> 00:35:17,550
We keep--
We keep him distracted.

603
00:35:17,637 --> 00:35:19,595
And we buy your father time.

604
00:35:19,682 --> 00:35:22,076
And while we wait
and be polite and

605
00:35:22,163 --> 00:35:25,775
kowtow to that,
any peace we buy

606
00:35:25,862 --> 00:35:27,255
is not guaranteed.

607
00:35:27,342 --> 00:35:28,909
It is all we can do.

608
00:35:30,302 --> 00:35:31,172
For now.

609
00:35:39,311 --> 00:35:41,443
Now, come on.

610
00:35:41,530 --> 00:35:43,358
We mustn’t keep our
honored guest waiting.

611
00:35:46,796 --> 00:35:48,407
Steady there, Alkimos.

612
00:35:48,494 --> 00:35:50,931
With any luck, we’ll be home
in a day and a half.

613
00:35:54,413 --> 00:35:55,327
My king.

614
00:35:59,896 --> 00:36:01,681
A storm’s coming.

615
00:36:01,768 --> 00:36:02,595
Keep it steady, Alkimos.

616
00:36:02,682 --> 00:36:04,466
Put your back into it.

617
00:36:04,553 --> 00:36:06,381
Polites, grab those lines.
We’re going to need them.

618
00:36:09,341 --> 00:36:11,647
Poseidon’s bad breath.

619
00:36:11,734 --> 00:36:13,562
Poseidon’s breath?

620
00:36:13,649 --> 00:36:15,651
Hot air and spit.

621
00:36:15,738 --> 00:36:18,306
That’s Poseidon’s bad breath.

622
00:36:18,393 --> 00:36:21,353
He must have learned of
the mark I left on his son.

623
00:36:21,440 --> 00:36:23,703
And his ocean is
pushing us off course.

624
00:36:23,790 --> 00:36:24,704
Look, there.

625
00:36:30,057 --> 00:36:31,972
Oh, yes.

626
00:36:32,059 --> 00:36:34,844
The current.
It turns against us.

627
00:36:34,931 --> 00:36:37,412
Yes.
Adjust your heading.

628
00:36:37,499 --> 00:36:39,109
Into the wind.

629
00:36:39,197 --> 00:36:40,241
- The wind?
- Yes.

630
00:36:40,328 --> 00:36:42,200
My king...

631
00:36:42,287 --> 00:36:44,767
Poseidon’s strength is strongest
in the water and the current.

632
00:36:46,508 --> 00:36:49,250
But if we catch the wind
we can use it as our guide.

633
00:36:49,337 --> 00:36:52,079
Polites, fortify the mast
and the sail.

634
00:36:54,690 --> 00:36:58,041
We cannot afford to be
any further from our goal.

635
00:36:58,128 --> 00:37:01,219
My king, the ship...

636
00:37:01,306 --> 00:37:02,655
She can’t hold it.

637
00:37:02,742 --> 00:37:05,223
She will do what
we tell her to do.

638
00:37:05,310 --> 00:37:07,268
The wind going one way
and the current the other.

639
00:37:07,355 --> 00:37:09,096
I’m afraid we’re
going to be torn apart.

640
00:37:09,183 --> 00:37:11,751
If we catch the wind,
tread lightly.

641
00:37:11,838 --> 00:37:14,754
We can crest the waves
instead of wrestling with them.

642
00:37:17,409 --> 00:37:19,149
Yes.

643
00:37:19,237 --> 00:37:21,630
We must tread lightly if we
want to keep pace with those.

644
00:37:23,763 --> 00:37:27,723
Spit all you want, Poseidon!
I will not be swayed.

645
00:37:27,810 --> 00:37:29,116
Not today, not ever.

646
00:37:30,422 --> 00:37:31,814
Odysseus...

647
00:37:34,730 --> 00:37:36,471
The waves!

648
00:37:36,558 --> 00:37:37,603
The waves, they’re coming
right for us.

649
00:37:46,786 --> 00:37:49,049
We’ve got
to keep her on course!

650
00:37:49,136 --> 00:37:50,790
Try harder.

651
00:37:50,877 --> 00:37:54,489
Come on!
Come on, oh gods. Punish me!

652
00:37:54,576 --> 00:37:55,708
Punish me!

653
00:38:00,103 --> 00:38:02,062
The sea,
she means to turn us over!

654
00:38:04,760 --> 00:38:06,501
- Odysseus.
- Hold tight.

655
00:38:06,588 --> 00:38:08,416
Hold tight, men.
We can steer her.

656
00:38:18,078 --> 00:38:19,427
We’re not strong enough.

657
00:38:19,514 --> 00:38:21,255
We’re not going to make it.

658
00:38:21,342 --> 00:38:23,605
The sea shall eat us all.

659
00:38:23,692 --> 00:38:25,955
The wind.
The wind’s too strong.

660
00:38:26,042 --> 00:38:27,957
We’re going to lose the sail.

661
00:38:28,044 --> 00:38:30,743
No. No, it’s not too strong.
It’s the strength we need.

662
00:38:32,005 --> 00:38:33,659
Hand me those lines.

663
00:38:41,014 --> 00:38:44,017
Pull the oar.
Pull with all your might!

664
00:38:44,104 --> 00:38:45,410
Pull!

665
00:38:45,497 --> 00:38:47,020
If we lose the oar,
we’re done for.

666
00:38:47,107 --> 00:38:50,023
If we do not act now, we die.

667
00:38:50,110 --> 00:38:51,198
Pull the oar!

668
00:38:55,681 --> 00:38:56,682
Pull it!

669
00:38:59,162 --> 00:39:02,252
That’s it. That’s it!

670
00:39:02,340 --> 00:39:03,993
The wind is the
strength we need.

671
00:39:09,869 --> 00:39:10,739
It’s working.

672
00:39:24,013 --> 00:39:26,494
Odysseus.

673
00:39:26,581 --> 00:39:27,539
Wind.

674
00:39:27,626 --> 00:39:29,671
Wind and water.

675
00:39:29,758 --> 00:39:31,978
They are nothing.

676
00:39:32,065 --> 00:39:34,415
They are nothing
when Ithaca waits for us.

677
00:39:39,986 --> 00:39:42,728
I’m coming home.

678
00:39:42,815 --> 00:39:44,251
I am coming home.

679
00:39:53,391 --> 00:39:55,654
So then...

680
00:39:55,741 --> 00:39:57,090
Queen Penelope.

681
00:39:58,483 --> 00:40:00,485
Antinous.

682
00:40:00,572 --> 00:40:02,356
Do you have need of more food?

683
00:40:02,443 --> 00:40:03,966
Or drink perhaps?

684
00:40:07,056 --> 00:40:09,407
Both. Please.

685
00:40:11,844 --> 00:40:13,846
Your wine.

686
00:40:13,933 --> 00:40:15,761
It has a lovely bouquet.

687
00:40:20,287 --> 00:40:22,942
A noble vintage indeed.

688
00:40:23,029 --> 00:40:25,379
A captivating aroma.

689
00:40:25,466 --> 00:40:27,773
And sweet acidity.

690
00:40:27,860 --> 00:40:29,731
It is rich.

691
00:40:29,818 --> 00:40:31,254
And full bodied.

692
00:40:34,301 --> 00:40:35,650
Just like you.

693
00:40:37,652 --> 00:40:39,437
You do flatter me, Antinous.

694
00:40:41,569 --> 00:40:42,701
Maybe I do.

695
00:40:44,920 --> 00:40:46,835
But...

696
00:40:46,922 --> 00:40:48,620
I worry...

697
00:40:48,707 --> 00:40:52,624
The time for flattery
has passed.

698
00:40:52,711 --> 00:40:55,409
There will be other suitors

699
00:40:55,496 --> 00:40:58,107
lining up at your door.

700
00:40:58,194 --> 00:41:00,370
And soon...

701
00:41:00,458 --> 00:41:04,636
They will be much
less patient men than I.

702
00:41:04,723 --> 00:41:06,681
And after the suitors

703
00:41:06,768 --> 00:41:08,378
will come the looters.

704
00:41:08,466 --> 00:41:09,989
And then the troublemakers.

705
00:41:11,947 --> 00:41:14,080
And I will be unable
to protect you.

706
00:41:15,821 --> 00:41:17,083
Unless?

707
00:41:17,170 --> 00:41:19,302
Unless I am your husband.

708
00:41:20,869 --> 00:41:22,480
Unless I am king.

709
00:41:25,700 --> 00:41:29,965
I would hate to see anything
unfortunate befall you...

710
00:41:31,097 --> 00:41:34,666
Or your son in such

711
00:41:34,753 --> 00:41:36,929
rudderless times as these.

712
00:41:39,279 --> 00:41:42,108
It is time to make
your choice, Penelope.

713
00:41:45,677 --> 00:41:47,896
Before someone makes it for you.

714
00:42:12,312 --> 00:42:15,445
Alkimos, get some beeswax.

715
00:42:15,533 --> 00:42:18,100
Focus repairs on
the prow of the ship.

716
00:42:18,187 --> 00:42:20,712
That storm really took
a bite out of our hide.

717
00:42:20,799 --> 00:42:23,149
But we need the ship
to have her speed back.

718
00:42:23,236 --> 00:42:24,237
Yes, my king.

719
00:42:26,761 --> 00:42:30,983
The sea herself added another
day and a half to our journey

720
00:42:31,070 --> 00:42:33,899
that we do not have to spare.

721
00:42:33,986 --> 00:42:36,118
The woes of my
heart upon this sea.

722
00:42:38,599 --> 00:42:40,688
What storm do you
think was worse?

723
00:42:40,775 --> 00:42:42,690
The one we just sailed through,
or the one that is on your brow?

724
00:42:44,779 --> 00:42:46,520
The storm to come.

725
00:42:46,607 --> 00:42:48,174
The journey ahead.

726
00:42:48,261 --> 00:42:49,131
The journey home.

727
00:42:50,524 --> 00:42:52,352
Yes.

728
00:42:52,439 --> 00:42:55,311
Polites, is I must get home
no matter what the cost.

729
00:42:55,398 --> 00:42:56,791
I must.

730
00:42:56,878 --> 00:42:58,924
I wonder if the costs
already paid.

731
00:42:59,011 --> 00:43:00,534
No, Polites...

732
00:43:00,621 --> 00:43:03,102
The costs were paid
both here and back in Troy.

733
00:43:04,494 --> 00:43:06,801
With flesh and blood.

734
00:43:06,888 --> 00:43:08,194
And this.
This is our reward, is it?

735
00:43:08,281 --> 00:43:10,544
More hardship.

736
00:43:10,631 --> 00:43:12,502
More storms.
Rough seas.

737
00:43:12,590 --> 00:43:14,896
I cannot control the fates.
Nor the seas.

738
00:43:14,983 --> 00:43:16,158
You forget that
I’ve seen you

739
00:43:16,245 --> 00:43:17,899
perform miracles
before, Odysseus.

740
00:43:17,986 --> 00:43:19,945
Miracles even
the gods couldn’t choose.

741
00:43:20,032 --> 00:43:22,034
If this was my kingdom, yes.

742
00:43:22,121 --> 00:43:23,905
But this is not my kingdom.

743
00:43:23,992 --> 00:43:25,690
Back home in my kingdom--

744
00:43:25,777 --> 00:43:26,734
By the gods of the sea!

745
00:43:32,044 --> 00:43:32,958
The sea has teeth!

746
00:43:40,139 --> 00:43:41,183
By the gods!

747
00:43:41,270 --> 00:43:43,272
Charybdis!

748
00:43:43,359 --> 00:43:46,145
It’s another one of Poseidon’s
weapons of vengeance.

749
00:43:46,232 --> 00:43:48,190
You didn’t stop Poseidon.

750
00:43:48,277 --> 00:43:49,583
You just made him more angry.

751
00:43:54,327 --> 00:43:56,982
Alkimos, come steer.

752
00:43:57,069 --> 00:43:58,723
Steer us around
the outside of it.

753
00:43:58,810 --> 00:44:00,899
My king, the repairs
aren’t finished yet.

754
00:44:00,986 --> 00:44:02,814
Without them, we won’t
be able to make that turn.

755
00:44:02,901 --> 00:44:04,076
It will be almost impossible.

756
00:44:04,163 --> 00:44:06,600
I will make that turn.
I will.

757
00:44:22,094 --> 00:44:23,356
The sea!

758
00:44:23,443 --> 00:44:24,574
It has swallowed so many,

759
00:44:24,662 --> 00:44:26,054
but I had no idea
it had a mouth!

760
00:44:28,100 --> 00:44:30,102
My king, the winds!

761
00:44:30,189 --> 00:44:31,059
We’re losing speed!

762
00:44:33,409 --> 00:44:35,107
Odysseus, what do we do?

763
00:44:35,194 --> 00:44:38,414
We will not let it
dissuade us off our course.

764
00:44:38,501 --> 00:44:39,981
Push, Alkimos!

765
00:44:40,068 --> 00:44:41,069
Push, Polites!

766
00:44:41,156 --> 00:44:42,854
Push!

767
00:44:42,941 --> 00:44:46,422
We will ride the wind
and the waves to freedom!

768
00:44:46,509 --> 00:44:48,729
Aye, my king!

769
00:44:48,816 --> 00:44:49,687
Odysseus!

770
00:44:51,776 --> 00:44:52,690
Look!

771
00:44:55,997 --> 00:44:57,956
By the gods!
It follows!

772
00:44:59,914 --> 00:45:02,438
Use the gift, son of Laertes!
The gift!

773
00:45:02,525 --> 00:45:03,962
The gift the goddess
gave to you!

774
00:45:04,049 --> 00:45:05,615
If it can help us, use it!

775
00:45:05,703 --> 00:45:06,834
Soon, loyal Polites!

776
00:45:06,921 --> 00:45:08,662
Use it now!

777
00:45:08,749 --> 00:45:11,883
I will not let
Poseidon’s pet beat us.

778
00:45:11,970 --> 00:45:17,105
He will not have the glory of
knowing I took the easy way out.

779
00:45:17,192 --> 00:45:18,585
Move!

780
00:45:18,672 --> 00:45:19,673
He is right.

781
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:21,414
You should use it.

782
00:45:21,501 --> 00:45:24,852
It is too early in the game
for you to die, Odysseus.

783
00:45:24,939 --> 00:45:27,159
Your pride will be your ruin.

784
00:45:28,943 --> 00:45:33,165
You cannot win
this without help.

785
00:45:33,252 --> 00:45:36,081
I can beat this beast on my own.

786
00:45:36,168 --> 00:45:38,518
I do not need
your help, goddess.

787
00:45:38,605 --> 00:45:40,520
I will get home in
spite of you...

788
00:45:40,607 --> 00:45:42,391
and all the gods!

789
00:45:42,478 --> 00:45:45,525
I will waste no
more breath on you.

790
00:45:45,612 --> 00:45:49,137
Not when it can be used
for another purpose.

791
00:45:49,224 --> 00:45:50,835
My king...

792
00:45:50,922 --> 00:45:52,793
Who the hell is it
you’re talking to back there?

793
00:45:52,880 --> 00:45:55,013
I will beat this beast
without your help, goddess.

794
00:46:00,627 --> 00:46:02,629
The whirlpool!

795
00:46:02,716 --> 00:46:04,762
It’s coming!

796
00:46:04,849 --> 00:46:06,067
Better hold on to something.

797
00:46:08,374 --> 00:46:09,462
Hold on, men.

798
00:46:12,030 --> 00:46:14,293
It’s not just any whirlpool.

799
00:46:14,380 --> 00:46:16,382
It’s a creature of Poseidon.

800
00:46:16,469 --> 00:46:19,907
And if it’s hungry,
we must feed it.

801
00:46:19,994 --> 00:46:23,868
We must feed it
until its belly is full.

802
00:46:23,955 --> 00:46:24,999
Until it chokes!

803
00:46:25,086 --> 00:46:26,522
Come on!

804
00:46:26,609 --> 00:46:28,873
What do you mean feed it?
Feed it with what?

805
00:46:28,960 --> 00:46:32,441
Quick!
Or we’ve already lost.

806
00:46:38,099 --> 00:46:41,059
Yes, what the storm
has taken from us.

807
00:46:41,146 --> 00:46:42,625
Grab the ropes.

808
00:46:42,712 --> 00:46:44,453
Anything, planks, barrels.

809
00:46:44,540 --> 00:46:46,455
Grab anything you can.

810
00:46:46,542 --> 00:46:48,457
Absolute madness.
You went mad, old man.

811
00:46:53,071 --> 00:46:56,248
Feed this beast.
Fill its belly.

812
00:46:56,335 --> 00:46:58,119
And if not...

813
00:46:58,206 --> 00:47:00,774
I will choke it myself
with my own body.

814
00:47:04,560 --> 00:47:06,867
Looks like the sea must eat.

815
00:47:06,954 --> 00:47:08,651
The fishing traps.

816
00:47:08,738 --> 00:47:10,566
Yes, the provisions. Now!

817
00:47:10,653 --> 00:47:12,786
The fishing traps?
What are we going to eat?

818
00:47:12,873 --> 00:47:14,832
We don’t need them.

819
00:47:14,919 --> 00:47:16,877
We’ll be home in two days.

820
00:47:16,964 --> 00:47:19,880
Or we’ll be dead
trying to protect it.

821
00:47:19,967 --> 00:47:21,273
Choke on it!

822
00:47:21,360 --> 00:47:22,578
Choke on all of it!

823
00:47:26,017 --> 00:47:27,975
It’s working!
It’s working!

824
00:47:30,151 --> 00:47:32,414
Now what we need is distance.
Move!

825
00:47:37,332 --> 00:47:38,943
Hear me, gods!

826
00:47:39,030 --> 00:47:42,381
I am strong enough
to fight my own battles.

827
00:47:42,468 --> 00:47:44,862
Damn you! Damn you!

828
00:47:56,786 --> 00:47:58,876
Gods.

829
00:47:58,963 --> 00:48:01,095
Please, please help us.

830
00:48:02,662 --> 00:48:05,273
Father...

831
00:48:05,360 --> 00:48:07,449
Father, why have
you abandoned us?

832
00:48:12,802 --> 00:48:14,630
Odysseus is alive.

833
00:48:14,717 --> 00:48:16,937
Who... who are you?

834
00:48:17,024 --> 00:48:19,592
Save your breath, child.

835
00:48:19,679 --> 00:48:20,941
Do not fear me.

836
00:48:22,856 --> 00:48:26,425
How have you appeared like that?

837
00:48:26,512 --> 00:48:28,122
Is this some magician’s trick?

838
00:48:28,209 --> 00:48:29,863
A phantasm?

839
00:48:29,950 --> 00:48:32,431
Please do not vex me, spirit!

840
00:48:32,518 --> 00:48:35,086
Athena hears your prayers.

841
00:48:35,173 --> 00:48:36,957
And you must hear my words.

842
00:48:39,046 --> 00:48:42,615
Your father has
not abandoned you.

843
00:48:42,702 --> 00:48:45,183
And neither have the gods.

844
00:48:45,270 --> 00:48:46,967
I am doing all that I can.

845
00:48:49,230 --> 00:48:52,233
Never before have I
seen a man fight for his home

846
00:48:52,320 --> 00:48:56,585
so boldly as
your father does in war.

847
00:48:56,672 --> 00:48:58,457
He honors me.

848
00:48:58,544 --> 00:49:02,417
And for that, I am
doing all that I can.

849
00:49:04,071 --> 00:49:08,989
But now, my child,
I must ask the same of you.

850
00:49:09,076 --> 00:49:11,861
You must protect
your father’s house

851
00:49:11,949 --> 00:49:16,388
at all costs, from
within and from without.

852
00:49:16,475 --> 00:49:21,610
You must be prepared
to fight in war, yes.

853
00:49:21,697 --> 00:49:26,311
But you must also be careful,
watchful, and cunning.

854
00:49:28,530 --> 00:49:30,968
It is time for boys
to become men.

855
00:49:40,890 --> 00:49:42,980
I see Odysseus in you.

856
00:49:45,156 --> 00:49:46,853
Perhaps it will be enough.

857
00:49:50,900 --> 00:49:52,728
Yes.

858
00:49:52,815 --> 00:49:55,427
Perhaps it will be enough.

859
00:49:55,514 --> 00:49:58,517
Be vigilant, Telemachus.

860
00:49:58,604 --> 00:50:01,172
You will know it when
the moment comes.

861
00:50:04,479 --> 00:50:07,439
And then you
will strike...

862
00:50:07,526 --> 00:50:12,357
for your mother,
for your father, and for me.

863
00:50:17,014 --> 00:50:19,103
A toast, then.

864
00:50:19,190 --> 00:50:21,627
A toast to our health,

865
00:50:21,714 --> 00:50:27,546
and in gratitude to
my most gracious hosts.

866
00:50:27,633 --> 00:50:29,722
Who, if I might
speak freely,

867
00:50:29,809 --> 00:50:34,335
deserve a king who would
protect them at home,

868
00:50:34,422 --> 00:50:36,598
and not seek glory elsewhere.

869
00:50:39,079 --> 00:50:41,734
And I most certainly
could be that king.

870
00:50:44,650 --> 00:50:48,784
A kingdom so fine
deserves a ruler

871
00:50:48,871 --> 00:50:51,396
who would never
think to abandon it.

872
00:50:51,483 --> 00:50:54,181
This is the king’s house.

873
00:50:54,268 --> 00:50:55,530
Odysseus’s house.

874
00:50:55,617 --> 00:50:56,705
My father’s house.

875
00:50:57,967 --> 00:50:59,360
Ah.

876
00:50:59,447 --> 00:51:03,669
And where exactly is
your father, Telemachus?

877
00:51:03,756 --> 00:51:05,410
I don’t see him.

878
00:51:05,497 --> 00:51:09,414
All I see instead
is a home deserted,

879
00:51:09,501 --> 00:51:14,158
a kingdom undefended,
vulnerable,

880
00:51:14,245 --> 00:51:17,683
and of the king
himself some stories.

881
00:51:17,770 --> 00:51:20,338
The poets are talking
out on the street.

882
00:51:20,425 --> 00:51:24,385
Some say he’s lost at sea.

883
00:51:24,472 --> 00:51:29,129
Others, that he dallies
with Cersei herself.

884
00:51:29,216 --> 00:51:32,828
And I myself have heard
nothing but stories of

885
00:51:32,915 --> 00:51:36,049
monsters and treasure

886
00:51:36,136 --> 00:51:39,139
and violence
and such bloodshed.

887
00:51:42,664 --> 00:51:46,929
Whatever stories you’ve heard,
you should know this:

888
00:51:47,016 --> 00:51:52,152
while my father is gone
and in his absence,

889
00:51:52,239 --> 00:51:56,287
I am bound to
defend what is his.

890
00:52:01,248 --> 00:52:04,425
Well, if it’s a fight
you’re looking for, boy.

891
00:52:04,512 --> 00:52:07,646
Honorable Antinous.

892
00:52:07,733 --> 00:52:11,128
Strong words do harm
to the digestion.

893
00:52:11,215 --> 00:52:14,827
And violence is an ill
guest in this house or any.

894
00:52:20,049 --> 00:52:22,313
Please.

895
00:52:22,400 --> 00:52:23,792
Of course, my lady.

896
00:52:25,794 --> 00:52:27,405
Of course.

897
00:52:27,492 --> 00:52:33,237
But I wouldn’t need to
be a guest if my queen

898
00:52:33,324 --> 00:52:35,239
would take this suitor
to be her husband.

899
00:52:35,326 --> 00:52:37,371
I fear I cannot remarry.

900
00:52:40,853 --> 00:52:42,507
What?

901
00:52:42,594 --> 00:52:45,205
I cannot remarry
until I finish this cloak

902
00:52:45,292 --> 00:52:48,817
I’ve been
making for my son.

903
00:52:48,904 --> 00:52:51,864
It is only custom and proper.

904
00:52:51,951 --> 00:52:55,302
A parting gift for my
only flesh and blood.

905
00:52:57,565 --> 00:53:03,092
Before my duties as queen
to my new king

906
00:53:03,180 --> 00:53:07,184
fill my days and my hand.

907
00:53:10,317 --> 00:53:12,232
Indeed.

908
00:53:12,319 --> 00:53:16,410
Now far be it from me
to rush propriety.

909
00:53:16,497 --> 00:53:21,067
And I surely look forward
to viewing the finished product.

910
00:53:41,392 --> 00:53:46,571
Must the entire sea move
against Odysseus, Lord Poseidon?

911
00:53:46,658 --> 00:53:49,965
Behavior from such a god
seems small and petty.

912
00:53:50,052 --> 00:53:51,271
This isn’t petty.

913
00:53:51,358 --> 00:53:53,360
This is revenge!

914
00:53:53,447 --> 00:53:57,146
For my son, for what
Odysseus did to my son.

915
00:53:57,234 --> 00:54:03,152
Your son, Lord Poseidon, was
harming and murdering innocents.

916
00:54:03,240 --> 00:54:06,895
Perhaps he will
think twice next time.

917
00:54:06,982 --> 00:54:09,811
Enjoy your pet hero
while you can, Athena.

918
00:54:09,898 --> 00:54:11,073
With your gifts.

919
00:54:11,160 --> 00:54:12,553
Your meddling matters not.

920
00:54:12,640 --> 00:54:16,165
Odysseus will not
leave my ocean alive.

921
00:54:27,046 --> 00:54:29,091
Polites, how is she?

922
00:54:29,178 --> 00:54:30,789
Is she patched up yet?

923
00:54:30,876 --> 00:54:32,660
She’s looking pretty good.

924
00:54:32,747 --> 00:54:35,141
Pretty good,
a little bit more...

925
00:54:35,228 --> 00:54:36,751
beeswax and
she’ll be good to go.

926
00:54:36,838 --> 00:54:39,058
Good, good.

927
00:54:39,145 --> 00:54:41,539
Set a course for home, Alkimos.

928
00:54:41,626 --> 00:54:43,149
Yes, my king.

929
00:54:43,236 --> 00:54:46,848
For home, and for our rewards.

930
00:54:46,935 --> 00:54:49,242
Gold.

931
00:54:49,329 --> 00:54:53,420
Yes, yes, indeed, Polites.
Indeed.

932
00:54:53,507 --> 00:54:55,727
And just on that,
by the way, I’ve been meaning

933
00:54:55,814 --> 00:54:56,945
to tell you, meaning
to ask about...

934
00:54:57,032 --> 00:54:58,077
Odysseus!

935
00:55:01,820 --> 00:55:03,212
A monster!

936
00:55:03,300 --> 00:55:05,302
The Scylla!
Poseidon’s lap dog!

937
00:55:06,955 --> 00:55:08,217
Alkimos, no!

938
00:55:12,396 --> 00:55:13,310
Odysseus!

939
00:55:19,794 --> 00:55:22,231
What do we do?

940
00:55:22,319 --> 00:55:23,842
We try to outrun it.

941
00:55:23,929 --> 00:55:25,104
The wind...

942
00:55:25,191 --> 00:55:26,497
The wind will guide us.

943
00:55:31,415 --> 00:55:33,852
Yes... Yes, faster!

944
00:55:33,939 --> 00:55:35,070
Faster!

945
00:55:42,600 --> 00:55:43,731
No!

946
00:55:47,866 --> 00:55:50,521
No! There’s not enough wind
at our back!

947
00:55:51,783 --> 00:55:54,786
Not enough wind to fight!

948
00:55:54,873 --> 00:55:56,440
Only the monster at our heels!

949
00:55:56,527 --> 00:55:57,832
Damn my pride!

950
00:56:02,881 --> 00:56:05,623
Athena! Athena!

951
00:56:07,059 --> 00:56:08,277
I’m asking for help!

952
00:56:13,848 --> 00:56:15,328
Another gift, then?

953
00:56:15,415 --> 00:56:17,156
Goddess!
We need weapons!

954
00:56:19,288 --> 00:56:21,508
Ask, and it will be yours.

955
00:56:21,595 --> 00:56:24,816
Strategy. And sight.

956
00:56:24,903 --> 00:56:26,252
Sight.

957
00:56:26,339 --> 00:56:27,645
That will be my second gift.

958
00:56:29,429 --> 00:56:30,778
To see what is
right in front of you.

959
00:56:31,997 --> 00:56:33,607
What is right in front of me?

960
00:56:33,694 --> 00:56:35,566
What? What does that mean?

961
00:56:40,179 --> 00:56:42,355
My ship.

962
00:56:42,442 --> 00:56:46,403
You ask for a weapon,
and yet you sail one.

963
00:56:46,490 --> 00:56:47,926
Yes.

964
00:56:48,013 --> 00:56:50,711
Why didn’t I see that before?

965
00:56:50,798 --> 00:56:51,886
The ship is a weapon.

966
00:56:55,629 --> 00:56:58,415
Sometimes you are so
worried about what is ahead,

967
00:56:58,502 --> 00:57:01,983
that you fail to see what
is directly in front of you.

968
00:57:02,070 --> 00:57:03,768
Take heed, Odysseus.

969
00:57:03,855 --> 00:57:06,553
You will defeat the
monsters that surround you.

970
00:57:17,216 --> 00:57:18,609
Odysseus?

971
00:57:18,696 --> 00:57:20,611
Odysseus, you’re steering us...

972
00:57:20,698 --> 00:57:22,482
You steer us right towards it!

973
00:57:22,569 --> 00:57:24,092
Right towards the
jaws of the beast!

974
00:57:24,179 --> 00:57:26,617
Not the jaws!
It’s belly!

975
00:57:28,662 --> 00:57:30,534
We’ll run it through
with the prow!

976
00:57:30,621 --> 00:57:32,057
The ship will be our sword!

977
00:57:32,144 --> 00:57:34,276
Yes! Yes!

978
00:57:43,372 --> 00:57:45,418
Odysseus, it’s
not going to work!

979
00:57:45,505 --> 00:57:48,508
We’ve got to turn her
around or we’re done for!

980
00:57:48,595 --> 00:57:50,379
The current is too strong!

981
00:57:50,467 --> 00:57:51,598
It’s not working!

982
00:57:54,645 --> 00:57:57,125
She’s going to eat us alive!

983
00:57:57,212 --> 00:57:58,823
Hold fast, Polites!

984
00:57:58,910 --> 00:58:01,216
We need momentum!

985
00:58:01,303 --> 00:58:02,217
I’m trying!

986
00:58:18,407 --> 00:58:20,801
That’s the only
belly-full you’ll get today!

987
00:58:20,888 --> 00:58:22,063
Yes, Odysseus!

988
00:58:27,547 --> 00:58:28,548
Yes!

989
00:58:34,554 --> 00:58:36,077
Come on, Telemachus.

990
00:58:36,164 --> 00:58:38,906
You must be ready to fight!

991
00:58:40,778 --> 00:58:41,648
Or we’ll lose everything.

992
00:58:44,521 --> 00:58:46,871
Come home, father.
Quickly.

993
00:58:57,969 --> 00:59:00,972
The cloak you make
for me is nearly finished.

994
00:59:01,059 --> 00:59:04,715
And there are unwanted
guests in your house.

995
00:59:04,802 --> 00:59:06,586
About the cloak...

996
00:59:06,673 --> 00:59:08,066
Alas, my son.

997
00:59:08,153 --> 00:59:11,417
I fear there is still
so much work to be done.

998
00:59:11,504 --> 00:59:14,551
This cloak may take
many days to finish.

999
00:59:16,378 --> 00:59:18,163
You’re undoing what you’ve done.

1000
00:59:18,250 --> 00:59:19,381
If only I could.

1001
00:59:21,383 --> 00:59:23,385
Mother, you’ve
done nothing wrong.

1002
00:59:23,472 --> 00:59:24,865
It’s just...

1003
00:59:24,952 --> 00:59:26,780
What?

1004
00:59:26,867 --> 00:59:28,695
Undoing the cloak every night.

1005
00:59:31,306 --> 00:59:32,743
Sometimes clever
is better than good.

1006
00:59:36,311 --> 00:59:39,010
I just pray it buys
us the time he needs.

1007
00:59:39,097 --> 00:59:41,142
And if it doesn’t, then
I will be ready to fight.

1008
00:59:42,883 --> 00:59:45,190
No one is taking
this kingdom from us.

1009
00:59:46,844 --> 00:59:47,714
No one.

1010
00:59:49,455 --> 00:59:50,761
And I will be ready to fight.

1011
01:00:08,866 --> 01:00:09,736
Odysseus?

1012
01:00:11,782 --> 01:00:13,740
Odysseus.

1013
01:00:13,827 --> 01:00:17,309
Treacherous waters ahead.

1014
01:00:17,396 --> 01:00:19,833
♪ Come to us

1015
01:00:19,920 --> 01:00:21,400
That’s treacherous
waters indeed.

1016
01:00:21,487 --> 01:00:23,054
It means we’re getting
close to land now.

1017
01:00:23,141 --> 01:00:24,882
Huh.

1018
01:00:24,969 --> 01:00:27,667
That’s the first good
thing I’ve heard all day.

1019
01:00:27,754 --> 01:00:29,451
We must stay clear
of those rocks.

1020
01:00:29,538 --> 01:00:31,671
And I have less
than a day before...

1021
01:00:31,758 --> 01:00:34,195
Yeah, before someone claims
what is rightfully yours.

1022
01:00:34,282 --> 01:00:36,894
I know, my king.
I know.

1023
01:00:36,981 --> 01:00:37,982
I am no king here.

1024
01:00:39,810 --> 01:00:41,376
There is only one
king in the ocean.

1025
01:00:41,463 --> 01:00:43,291
And I hold no favor
with him now.

1026
01:00:43,378 --> 01:00:44,815
♪ Come to us

1027
01:00:44,902 --> 01:00:46,120
Odysseus...

1028
01:00:46,207 --> 01:00:48,296
Can you hear that?

1029
01:00:48,383 --> 01:00:50,385
- Music?
- No.

1030
01:00:50,472 --> 01:00:52,083
No, not the songs...

1031
01:00:52,170 --> 01:00:53,606
of the sirens, no.

1032
01:00:53,693 --> 01:00:55,564
♪ Come to us

1033
01:00:58,655 --> 01:00:59,612
No.

1034
01:00:59,699 --> 01:01:01,570
Polites, no. No!

1035
01:01:01,658 --> 01:01:02,876
Protect yourself!

1036
01:01:02,963 --> 01:01:04,051
No!

1037
01:01:04,138 --> 01:01:05,749
- Sirens.
- Not the sirens!

1038
01:01:05,836 --> 01:01:06,967
They call to me.

1039
01:01:07,054 --> 01:01:09,143
No!

1040
01:01:09,230 --> 01:01:10,884
Cover your ears!

1041
01:01:10,971 --> 01:01:12,146
Protect yourself, Polites!

1042
01:01:16,847 --> 01:01:18,370
They speak to me.

1043
01:01:18,457 --> 01:01:20,502
Cover your ears!
Protect yourself!

1044
01:01:20,589 --> 01:01:21,547
They want me to get my reward.

1045
01:01:23,810 --> 01:01:25,029
Rewards for a good man.

1046
01:01:25,116 --> 01:01:27,988
No. No!

1047
01:01:28,075 --> 01:01:29,163
They speak to me.

1048
01:01:32,036 --> 01:01:35,430
I hear the song.
It’s in my head!

1049
01:01:35,517 --> 01:01:36,431
They speak to me.

1050
01:01:41,872 --> 01:01:45,658
Telemachus is the
real problem here.

1051
01:01:45,745 --> 01:01:49,575
The woman?
I can handle.

1052
01:01:49,662 --> 01:01:50,924
But the boy?

1053
01:01:53,231 --> 01:01:58,366
Any loyal dog would
struggle obeying a new master.

1054
01:01:58,453 --> 01:02:00,281
And I wouldn’t blame him.

1055
01:02:00,368 --> 01:02:04,024
But once this is all mine,

1056
01:02:04,111 --> 01:02:07,636
he will still be a
thorn in my side.

1057
01:02:07,724 --> 01:02:11,249
And his mother falling
into line won’t help.

1058
01:02:11,336 --> 01:02:13,251
No.

1059
01:02:13,338 --> 01:02:17,168
He’s going to require
a far stronger hand.

1060
01:02:17,255 --> 01:02:19,083
It’s not a matter of if.

1061
01:02:19,170 --> 01:02:22,477
Simply put, the question
becomes do I take care

1062
01:02:22,564 --> 01:02:26,699
of Telemachus now, or do
I take care of him later?

1063
01:02:30,529 --> 01:02:33,271
It’s like my father used to say.

1064
01:02:33,358 --> 01:02:37,579
Only a fool leaves
today’s jobs until tomorrow.

1065
01:02:37,666 --> 01:02:42,802
Well, Antinous is no fool.

1066
01:02:46,197 --> 01:02:47,807
Neither will this loyal dog.

1067
01:02:54,205 --> 01:02:56,120
No.

1068
01:02:56,207 --> 01:02:58,209
This loyal dog will be
ready for you, Antinous.

1069
01:03:06,783 --> 01:03:09,089
My reward...

1070
01:03:09,176 --> 01:03:10,047
Speak to me.

1071
01:03:11,875 --> 01:03:13,659
Odysseus!
I need to get my rewards!

1072
01:03:13,746 --> 01:03:15,356
What?
What are you doing?

1073
01:03:15,443 --> 01:03:17,881
My rewards, my gold!
They’re speaking to me!

1074
01:03:17,968 --> 01:03:19,447
- Not when we’re so close!
- Yes!

1075
01:03:19,534 --> 01:03:21,580
No! Just pass the rocks,
and we’re nearly home!

1076
01:03:21,667 --> 01:03:22,929
They speak to me!

1077
01:03:23,016 --> 01:03:24,670
Polites!
Polites!

1078
01:03:24,757 --> 01:03:26,411
Wake up!

1079
01:03:26,498 --> 01:03:27,847
Take this beeswax!

1080
01:03:27,934 --> 01:03:28,848
Put it in your ears!

1081
01:03:28,935 --> 01:03:30,763
We must block their song!

1082
01:03:30,850 --> 01:03:33,374
We must block
the siren’s song!

1083
01:03:33,461 --> 01:03:34,419
Block it!

1084
01:03:34,506 --> 01:03:36,551
We cannot hear them!

1085
01:03:36,638 --> 01:03:37,901
We’ve got to stay clear of them!

1086
01:03:37,988 --> 01:03:39,163
We’ve got to stay away!

1087
01:03:39,250 --> 01:03:40,555
Steady on the course...

1088
01:03:40,642 --> 01:03:42,819
until we can’t hear them!

1089
01:03:42,906 --> 01:03:44,211
Steer away...

1090
01:03:44,298 --> 01:03:45,212
Steer away.

1091
01:03:52,393 --> 01:03:54,308
That song...
That song...

1092
01:03:56,049 --> 01:03:57,529
Oh...

1093
01:03:57,616 --> 01:03:59,052
I want to hear that song.

1094
01:04:00,358 --> 01:04:01,663
Odysseus!

1095
01:04:05,276 --> 01:04:07,495
- That song...
- Odysseus!

1096
01:04:07,582 --> 01:04:09,715
Odysseus!
Don’t listen to them!

1097
01:04:09,802 --> 01:04:11,021
Here!

1098
01:04:11,108 --> 01:04:12,892
No, the wax!

1099
01:04:12,979 --> 01:04:13,850
The wax!

1100
01:04:14,981 --> 01:04:16,722
The song...

1101
01:04:16,809 --> 01:04:20,552
Odysseus! We’re leaving!
We’re getting out of here!

1102
01:04:20,639 --> 01:04:22,032
I want to hear that song.

1103
01:04:24,121 --> 01:04:25,383
They know nothing but death!

1104
01:04:25,470 --> 01:04:27,211
- They want to help us.
- They do not.

1105
01:04:27,298 --> 01:04:29,387
- They do want to help us.
- They don’t want to help us.

1106
01:04:29,474 --> 01:04:31,171
- Polites...
- Think of your family!

1107
01:04:31,258 --> 01:04:33,304
- Polites, they want to help us.
- No, no, no!

1108
01:04:33,391 --> 01:04:34,522
They will not!

1109
01:04:34,609 --> 01:04:35,697
Polites, they know
how to get home.

1110
01:04:35,784 --> 01:04:37,134
We’ve come this far!

1111
01:04:37,221 --> 01:04:38,570
- Polites...
- We’ve come this far!

1112
01:04:38,657 --> 01:04:39,658
- They only know death!
- They have wisdom!

1113
01:04:39,745 --> 01:04:40,746
They only know death!

1114
01:04:42,182 --> 01:04:43,140
Their song is wisdom.

1115
01:04:43,227 --> 01:04:45,229
Hey! Snap out of it!

1116
01:04:45,316 --> 01:04:46,534
They want to help us.

1117
01:04:46,621 --> 01:04:48,623
They know what
lies beneath the waves.

1118
01:04:48,710 --> 01:04:49,755
I must hear this song.

1119
01:04:51,409 --> 01:04:53,237
Song of the sirens.

1120
01:04:53,324 --> 01:04:55,326
What a beautiful song.

1121
01:04:55,413 --> 01:04:56,588
Huh.

1122
01:05:00,984 --> 01:05:02,507
They want to help us get home.

1123
01:05:04,770 --> 01:05:06,728
They do.

1124
01:05:06,815 --> 01:05:07,729
They have knowledge.

1125
01:05:07,816 --> 01:05:10,907
Polites, they have wisdom.

1126
01:05:12,299 --> 01:05:13,561
- Odysseus...
- Their beautiful song.

1127
01:05:13,648 --> 01:05:15,041
I must hear it.

1128
01:05:15,128 --> 01:05:17,130
Odysseus, they know
nothing but death.

1129
01:05:19,437 --> 01:05:20,786
They have knowledge.

1130
01:05:20,873 --> 01:05:22,440
They know nothing but death.

1131
01:05:22,527 --> 01:05:23,615
Think of home.

1132
01:05:26,618 --> 01:05:28,489
The song.

1133
01:05:28,576 --> 01:05:31,014
No, Polites, we
must steer towards them.

1134
01:05:31,101 --> 01:05:32,058
Polites, they want to help us!

1135
01:05:32,145 --> 01:05:33,364
No, they don’t!

1136
01:05:33,451 --> 01:05:35,714
Yes!
Their song!

1137
01:05:38,717 --> 01:05:39,761
I want to hear their song...

1138
01:05:46,551 --> 01:05:49,510
Please, Polites.
They sing for me!

1139
01:05:49,597 --> 01:05:51,034
Odysseus!

1140
01:05:51,121 --> 01:05:53,340
Odysseus!
Hear me, Odysseus!

1141
01:05:55,516 --> 01:05:58,998
You must not listen
to them, Odysseus!

1142
01:05:59,085 --> 01:06:00,652
I can only hear their song.

1143
01:06:00,739 --> 01:06:02,175
Odysseus!

1144
01:06:02,262 --> 01:06:03,916
No!

1145
01:06:04,003 --> 01:06:05,439
You can’t hear me
over their song!

1146
01:06:06,527 --> 01:06:08,268
Odysseus!

1147
01:06:08,355 --> 01:06:11,445
You will die if you don’t
make it past these rocks!

1148
01:06:11,532 --> 01:06:16,276
And if you cannot hear me,
I cannot help you!

1149
01:06:16,363 --> 01:06:17,756
I cannot help you.

1150
01:06:20,106 --> 01:06:21,412
Odysseus!

1151
01:06:21,499 --> 01:06:22,804
Odysseus, no!

1152
01:06:26,765 --> 01:06:29,811
Polites, we must
steer away from the rocks.

1153
01:06:29,898 --> 01:06:31,335
Please steer away from them.

1154
01:06:35,774 --> 01:06:37,602
We must get to Ithaca.

1155
01:06:37,689 --> 01:06:39,038
They will give us
a hero’s welcome.

1156
01:06:39,125 --> 01:06:40,605
A king’s welcome.

1157
01:06:44,522 --> 01:06:46,176
Damn the sirens.

1158
01:06:46,263 --> 01:06:48,656
And damn these seas, too.

1159
01:06:48,743 --> 01:06:50,963
Polites, please forgive me.

1160
01:06:51,050 --> 01:06:53,922
You have saved
both of our lives.

1161
01:06:54,010 --> 01:06:56,273
It was an honor, my king.

1162
01:06:56,360 --> 01:06:58,623
But now our course is set.

1163
01:06:58,710 --> 01:07:00,538
You are almost home.

1164
01:07:00,625 --> 01:07:02,148
And that is your reward.

1165
01:07:03,802 --> 01:07:07,936
But mind, my king,
my reward is treasure.

1166
01:07:08,024 --> 01:07:10,330
You must listen to me.
You don’t understand.

1167
01:07:10,417 --> 01:07:12,680
No, no, no, I do understand.

1168
01:07:12,767 --> 01:07:15,118
You have a gift from the gods.

1169
01:07:15,205 --> 01:07:16,815
And Ithaca, now
that is your reward.

1170
01:07:16,902 --> 01:07:17,903
Now I am in pursuit of mine.

1171
01:07:17,990 --> 01:07:20,558
You said, your words.

1172
01:07:20,645 --> 01:07:22,125
What do you mean?

1173
01:07:22,212 --> 01:07:23,822
Gifts for good men.
Did you not?

1174
01:07:23,909 --> 01:07:25,693
Please, you do not understand.

1175
01:07:25,780 --> 01:07:28,783
No, I do understand. It’s you
that doesn’t understand.

1176
01:07:28,870 --> 01:07:30,829
I saw Athena
herself give it to you.

1177
01:07:30,916 --> 01:07:32,657
- I saw her.
- No, no.

1178
01:07:32,744 --> 01:07:34,050
A gift from the gods. Reward.

1179
01:07:34,137 --> 01:07:35,573
No, it’s not a gift.

1180
01:07:35,660 --> 01:07:37,444
- Oh, it’s not a gift?
- No, it’s not.

1181
01:07:37,531 --> 01:07:38,793
I see it.

1182
01:07:38,880 --> 01:07:41,013
I see, clever Polites,
loyal Polites,

1183
01:07:41,100 --> 01:07:42,667
worthy of scars Polites,

1184
01:07:42,754 --> 01:07:45,626
but not worthy of a gift
from the gods, is that it?

1185
01:07:45,713 --> 01:07:47,889
Please listen to me.
Please listen to me.

1186
01:07:47,976 --> 01:07:49,674
Exactly like Troy, is it?

1187
01:07:49,761 --> 01:07:52,068
Exactly like Troy.

1188
01:07:52,155 --> 01:07:53,460
I am going to take
what is owed to me.

1189
01:07:53,547 --> 01:07:54,983
For good men.

1190
01:07:55,071 --> 01:07:58,726
No, no, do not take that.
Do not take that!

1191
01:07:58,813 --> 01:08:00,424
Bring it back.
You do not understand.

1192
01:08:00,511 --> 01:08:02,295
- No, stop.
- I do understand.

1193
01:08:02,382 --> 01:08:04,863
Stop!
Polites, please, please.

1194
01:08:04,950 --> 01:08:08,519
My King, I am only opening this

1195
01:08:08,606 --> 01:08:09,737
because this is
what is owed to me.

1196
01:08:09,824 --> 01:08:11,087
- No.
- Yes.

1197
01:08:22,576 --> 01:08:23,751
Athena.

1198
01:08:25,318 --> 01:08:27,451
Athena, please.

1199
01:08:57,307 --> 01:09:01,137
Perhaps the fates are more
generous than we thought.

1200
01:09:01,224 --> 01:09:02,747
Athena.

1201
01:09:02,834 --> 01:09:05,141
Even the winds had mercy
on you and brought you home.

1202
01:09:07,534 --> 01:09:09,188
Reward. Reward.

1203
01:09:12,452 --> 01:09:17,501
Great Odysseus finally returned
to the shores of Ithaca.

1204
01:09:18,850 --> 01:09:20,156
Ithaca.

1205
01:09:20,243 --> 01:09:24,464
Telemachus...
Penelope!

1206
01:09:24,551 --> 01:09:27,424
The great Odysseus...

1207
01:09:27,511 --> 01:09:29,730
At the end of his ropes.

1208
01:09:31,906 --> 01:09:33,778
No.

1209
01:09:33,865 --> 01:09:34,953
Not yet.
Not quite.

1210
01:09:36,607 --> 01:09:38,652
Look.

1211
01:09:38,739 --> 01:09:40,045
There, Your Majesty.

1212
01:09:46,312 --> 01:09:49,185
Your people are in panic.

1213
01:09:49,272 --> 01:09:51,752
Perhaps there is no more
glory left for you here.

1214
01:09:54,364 --> 01:09:58,063
Penelope and Telemachus
are doing all they can.

1215
01:09:58,150 --> 01:10:01,022
But your city is
being taken by force.

1216
01:10:01,109 --> 01:10:03,590
My world burns.

1217
01:10:03,677 --> 01:10:07,203
And the gods abandon me
with my hands tied.

1218
01:10:07,290 --> 01:10:09,379
I can offer no more aid,
and you know this.

1219
01:10:13,774 --> 01:10:16,516
By breathing wind
into your sails,

1220
01:10:16,603 --> 01:10:20,128
strategizing your
boat as a weapon,

1221
01:10:20,216 --> 01:10:22,783
and giving you a bag of wind,

1222
01:10:22,870 --> 01:10:26,961
your three gifts have been used.

1223
01:10:27,048 --> 01:10:30,704
But great goddess,
I never opened the bag of winds.

1224
01:10:30,791 --> 01:10:34,273
It was Polites.
Polites opened the bag of winds.

1225
01:10:34,360 --> 01:10:36,144
Not I.

1226
01:10:36,232 --> 01:10:37,450
A wrinkle.

1227
01:10:39,452 --> 01:10:43,630
Perhaps it is enough
for the gods to allow me

1228
01:10:43,717 --> 01:10:47,895
to aid you one last
time on your journey.

1229
01:10:47,982 --> 01:10:51,551
I have one boon.
One gift left.

1230
01:10:51,638 --> 01:10:53,901
I just pray I don’t
have to use it.

1231
01:10:53,988 --> 01:10:58,036
Yes, but you cannot
just go home, Odysseus.

1232
01:10:58,123 --> 01:11:00,734
Not anymore.

1233
01:11:00,821 --> 01:11:04,216
The people of Ithaca,
they are a mob.

1234
01:11:04,303 --> 01:11:06,871
They run through the
streets hungry for blood.

1235
01:11:06,958 --> 01:11:08,916
They would have yours.

1236
01:11:09,003 --> 01:11:11,136
You will not make it past them.

1237
01:11:11,223 --> 01:11:14,008
You will not even make it
to your front door.

1238
01:11:14,095 --> 01:11:18,186
And if you did, the suitors
would be waiting for you.

1239
01:11:20,145 --> 01:11:22,495
In disguise.

1240
01:11:22,582 --> 01:11:24,976
In disguise to hide
my face in my own kingdom.

1241
01:11:25,063 --> 01:11:26,804
I shall dress as a
wandering beggar

1242
01:11:26,891 --> 01:11:28,414
and learn how things stand.

1243
01:11:28,501 --> 01:11:31,461
What will you do,
Great Odysseus?

1244
01:11:31,548 --> 01:11:32,940
I will defend my home.

1245
01:11:33,027 --> 01:11:34,551
My family.

1246
01:11:34,638 --> 01:11:36,770
I must stop this
before it’s too late.

1247
01:11:36,857 --> 01:11:38,772
Or even if it’s as you say,

1248
01:11:38,859 --> 01:11:41,949
there’s no glory
left for either of us.

1249
01:11:43,821 --> 01:11:46,214
Finish the game, Odysseus.

1250
01:11:46,302 --> 01:11:48,391
And finish it well.

1251
01:11:48,478 --> 01:11:52,569
For not all gods
have abandoned you.

1252
01:11:52,656 --> 01:11:55,180
And Poseidon still
thirsts for revenge.

1253
01:12:04,537 --> 01:12:06,626
We must do something, Mother.

1254
01:12:06,713 --> 01:12:08,280
No.

1255
01:12:08,367 --> 01:12:09,716
I will not let them kill you.

1256
01:12:09,803 --> 01:12:11,501
You must get
control of the city.

1257
01:12:11,588 --> 01:12:15,069
We will wait until
your father returns.

1258
01:12:15,156 --> 01:12:16,462
Rioting!

1259
01:12:16,549 --> 01:12:18,464
Looting and violence,

1260
01:12:18,551 --> 01:12:21,249
and you want to wait for
Odysseus to return?

1261
01:12:23,251 --> 01:12:25,384
Do you still think
you’ll have a kingdom by then?

1262
01:12:29,736 --> 01:12:33,653
Without a king,
my queen,

1263
01:12:33,740 --> 01:12:38,789
the city succumbs to
its more primal urges.

1264
01:12:38,876 --> 01:12:42,575
I will take your hand
and the crown with it.

1265
01:12:42,662 --> 01:12:47,406
And all will be well
when King Antinous reigns.

1266
01:12:48,842 --> 01:12:51,236
It is time, my lord.

1267
01:12:51,323 --> 01:12:54,370
Time enough indeed
for the final challenge.

1268
01:12:57,721 --> 01:13:01,289
No more games, loyal widow.

1269
01:13:01,377 --> 01:13:03,683
Being king is no game.

1270
01:13:03,770 --> 01:13:05,206
And only the worthy
wear our crown

1271
01:13:05,293 --> 01:13:06,947
and complete this final task.

1272
01:13:09,689 --> 01:13:13,780
Only our king can fire an
arrow through those axe blades.

1273
01:13:13,867 --> 01:13:16,827
And only our king has done it.

1274
01:13:16,914 --> 01:13:21,092
So are you our king, Antinous?

1275
01:13:21,179 --> 01:13:26,793
Or does Odysseus, even in
his absence, carry the crown?

1276
01:13:26,880 --> 01:13:29,622
You’re not worth of such
a great feat!

1277
01:13:29,709 --> 01:13:31,189
I challenge you for
my Father’s kingdom!

1278
01:13:31,276 --> 01:13:32,799
Snake in the grass!

1279
01:13:35,280 --> 01:13:37,064
Stay in your place, pup!

1280
01:13:37,151 --> 01:13:39,153
Telemachus!

1281
01:13:39,240 --> 01:13:43,201
But if I must, I will
teach you to keep your place!

1282
01:13:44,898 --> 01:13:47,292
The crown will never be yours!

1283
01:13:49,773 --> 01:13:51,296
Snake in the grass!

1284
01:13:57,824 --> 01:14:00,218
Interloper! Villain!

1285
01:14:00,305 --> 01:14:01,872
Yes, I am.

1286
01:14:03,395 --> 01:14:06,224
And much worse.

1287
01:14:06,311 --> 01:14:08,008
Begging your apologies.

1288
01:14:08,095 --> 01:14:11,229
I was merely returning
the young man his sword.

1289
01:14:14,754 --> 01:14:16,713
I...

1290
01:14:16,800 --> 01:14:20,368
I would like to ask the young
lady if I may compete.

1291
01:14:20,456 --> 01:14:21,457
Another suitor?

1292
01:14:24,634 --> 01:14:26,200
With the queen’s
permission, of course.

1293
01:14:32,555 --> 01:14:34,513
We need no more suitors!

1294
01:14:34,600 --> 01:14:35,862
I will--

1295
01:14:35,949 --> 01:14:37,211
The courtesy of
my husband’s house

1296
01:14:37,298 --> 01:14:38,691
is much diminished
as of late.

1297
01:14:42,739 --> 01:14:44,697
You may compete.

1298
01:14:44,784 --> 01:14:47,787
And I pray you might
restore what has been lost.

1299
01:14:47,874 --> 01:14:51,922
Only Odysseus can put an
arrow through those axe blades.

1300
01:14:52,009 --> 01:14:56,100
So, let’s see how good you are.

1301
01:14:56,187 --> 01:14:57,101
Good.

1302
01:14:59,756 --> 01:15:02,323
Clever.

1303
01:15:02,410 --> 01:15:03,716
Clever is better than good.

1304
01:15:15,380 --> 01:15:17,469
It’s a farce!

1305
01:15:17,556 --> 01:15:20,211
He can’t even keep the
arrow straight, my queen.

1306
01:15:21,865 --> 01:15:24,868
Best keep your eye
on the target, old man.

1307
01:15:27,044 --> 01:15:30,438
So, only Odysseus
himself can make this shot.

1308
01:15:48,326 --> 01:15:50,850
And I must be Odysseus.

1309
01:15:50,937 --> 01:15:53,070
But Odysseus is dead!

1310
01:15:53,157 --> 01:15:55,159
- No!
- Father!

1311
01:15:57,770 --> 01:16:01,557
You have overstayed your
welcome in my house too long!

1312
01:16:01,644 --> 01:16:02,688
Antinous!

1313
01:16:04,734 --> 01:16:06,518
You can leave on
your own accord.

1314
01:16:06,605 --> 01:16:08,041
Never!

1315
01:16:08,128 --> 01:16:11,088
This kingdom is mine!

1316
01:16:11,175 --> 01:16:12,916
Or you can go another way!

1317
01:16:19,662 --> 01:16:23,666
Death is too good for you.

1318
01:16:23,753 --> 01:16:25,189
Watch your tongue!

1319
01:16:25,276 --> 01:16:27,887
Or we will take it from you.

1320
01:16:27,974 --> 01:16:29,149
No!

1321
01:16:36,026 --> 01:16:37,201
My gods!

1322
01:16:38,898 --> 01:16:40,117
Help me!

1323
01:16:40,204 --> 01:16:42,075
Have mercy on me!

1324
01:16:58,526 --> 01:17:00,006
Here I am, monster!

1325
01:17:05,098 --> 01:17:09,015
I’ll slaughter you and
your family,

1326
01:17:09,102 --> 01:17:11,583
and the whole of Ithaca!

1327
01:17:11,670 --> 01:17:13,019
No! No!

1328
01:17:13,106 --> 01:17:14,673
Here I am! Here I am!

1329
01:17:14,760 --> 01:17:16,370
I’m the one you want!

1330
01:17:16,457 --> 01:17:18,416
Take me! Take me!

1331
01:17:20,113 --> 01:17:21,201
Father!

1332
01:17:22,768 --> 01:17:23,769
Father!

1333
01:17:46,357 --> 01:17:47,358
Odysseus?

1334
01:17:49,142 --> 01:17:50,013
Odysseus!

1335
01:17:51,667 --> 01:17:53,059
No!

1336
01:17:53,146 --> 01:17:55,192
What did he say to you, son?

1337
01:17:55,279 --> 01:17:56,976
Tell me!
Tell me quickly!

1338
01:17:57,063 --> 01:17:58,543
What were his
final words to you?

1339
01:17:58,630 --> 01:18:00,284
I don’t understand!

1340
01:18:01,720 --> 01:18:02,634
Telemachus...

1341
01:18:02,721 --> 01:18:05,681
What were his final words?

1342
01:18:05,768 --> 01:18:08,945
He said, third gift.

1343
01:18:10,468 --> 01:18:11,382
Third gift?

1344
01:18:13,427 --> 01:18:14,341
And then...

1345
01:18:15,778 --> 01:18:16,909
And then nothing more.

1346
01:18:22,219 --> 01:18:23,394
So clever.

1347
01:18:24,787 --> 01:18:26,702
The king of Ithaca.

1348
01:18:26,789 --> 01:18:28,529
Clever to his last.

1349
01:18:30,314 --> 01:18:31,228
Oh, goddess Athena.

1350
01:18:32,925 --> 01:18:34,405
My love.

1351
01:18:34,492 --> 01:18:39,192
You have suffered
enough these past many days.

1352
01:18:39,279 --> 01:18:41,629
But the game’s over.

1353
01:18:41,717 --> 01:18:44,067
Odysseus...

1354
01:18:44,154 --> 01:18:45,329
Wins.

1355
01:18:45,416 --> 01:18:47,157
What do you mean?

1356
01:18:47,244 --> 01:18:48,811
He didn’t win.

1357
01:18:48,898 --> 01:18:50,595
He’s dead.
He’s gone!

1358
01:18:50,682 --> 01:18:54,425
Do not be troubled,
son of Odysseus.

1359
01:18:54,512 --> 01:18:58,124
Once again, your
father’s glory honors me.

1360
01:19:00,300 --> 01:19:02,650
With his final breath,

1361
01:19:02,738 --> 01:19:06,306
your husband requested his
last wish.

1362
01:19:09,222 --> 01:19:11,094
May I grant it?

1363
01:19:11,181 --> 01:19:12,051
Yes.

1364
01:19:15,489 --> 01:19:16,403
Yes, please.

1365
01:19:19,929 --> 01:19:23,541
Clever is indeed
greater than good.

1366
01:19:38,034 --> 01:19:39,644
- Father!
- He’s alive!

1367
01:19:42,038 --> 01:19:43,779
Goddess Athena, thank you.

1368
01:19:43,866 --> 01:19:46,303
It was your husband
who outsmarted death.

1369
01:19:48,174 --> 01:19:50,916
I am glad to see
you all reunited.

1370
01:19:59,969 --> 01:20:01,753
My son...

1371
01:20:01,840 --> 01:20:02,754
Oh, my son.

1372
01:20:05,061 --> 01:20:05,931
I was gone.

1373
01:20:07,498 --> 01:20:08,455
The goddess.

1374
01:20:11,545 --> 01:20:15,723
Athena has granted me
my third wish.

1375
01:20:17,725 --> 01:20:20,511
My father more clever
than the gods themselves.

1376
01:20:27,997 --> 01:20:30,434
The river Styx can wait...

1377
01:20:33,698 --> 01:20:35,091
But a moment longer.

1378
01:20:36,266 --> 01:20:37,136
Oh...

1379
01:20:38,790 --> 01:20:40,096
You took your time, old man.

1380
01:20:41,967 --> 01:20:43,534
The wind.

1381
01:20:43,621 --> 01:20:44,752
The wind was against me.

1382
01:20:47,016 --> 01:20:50,193
I have heard such stories.

1383
01:20:53,979 --> 01:20:57,113
You seem as if from a dream.

1384
01:20:57,200 --> 01:20:59,115
Are you really here?

1385
01:20:59,202 --> 01:21:01,857
Or are the gods
still playing games.

1386
01:21:04,468 --> 01:21:09,865
I have not the strength
for their sport any longer.

1387
01:21:09,952 --> 01:21:11,127
Nor do I.

1388
01:21:11,214 --> 01:21:12,650
Nor do I, my queen.

1389
01:21:16,045 --> 01:21:19,570
King of Ithaca, why have
you returned to this place?

1390
01:21:25,532 --> 01:21:29,058
I have but one goal.

1391
01:21:29,145 --> 01:21:30,015
One destination.

1392
01:21:33,497 --> 01:21:37,718
Not Ithaca, not this house,

1393
01:21:37,805 --> 01:21:39,459
not the crown was my goal.

1394
01:21:43,028 --> 01:21:46,292
I care not for the seas
or the skies.

1395
01:21:48,512 --> 01:21:51,819
I sought no monsters, nor gods.

1396
01:21:54,735 --> 01:21:56,302
I only sought you.

1397
01:21:58,739 --> 01:22:00,132
Only you.

1398
01:22:00,219 --> 01:22:05,572
You... You were the
safe harbor I fought

1399
01:22:05,659 --> 01:22:09,054
so desperately to get home to.

1400
01:22:18,281 --> 01:22:19,978
And home you will stay.

1401
01:22:21,458 --> 01:22:22,328
Yes, I will.

1402
01:22:26,854 --> 01:22:27,812
Home.

1403
01:22:30,206 --> 01:22:32,338
The only journey
worth the fight.

1404
01:22:38,997 --> 01:22:40,781
Home.

1405
01:22:40,868 --> 01:22:42,218
Home at last.
